window.BOOK_SEARCH = [{"title": "Axioms, inference rules, and proofs", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-1-1", "text": "1.1.1 Axioms, inference rules, and proofs § A proof starts from accepted assumptions and reaches a conclusion through allowed steps. The starting assumptions are called axioms . The allowed steps are specified by rules of inference . For example, if we know \\(P\\) and know that \\(P\\) implies \\(Q\\), we may conclude \\(Q\\). In the even-integer example from the introduction, each step used a definition or an arithmetic rule. If we record these justifications explicitly, another person can check the argument without having to discover it again. Now suppose the statements and rules are written precisely enough for each step to be checked by following fixed instructions. A machine could then check a supplied proof. This is the connection we need: reasoning can be represented as a finite sequence of operations. Each arrow shows a dependency between proof steps. The same inference rule first gives \\(Q\\) from \\(P\\), then \\(R\\) from \\(Q\\). Problems What is an axiom, and what is a rule of inference? Given \\(P\\) and “\\(P\\) implies \\(Q\\),” write the conclusion and identify the rule used. If a machine accepts every step of a proof, what has it established about the conclusion—and what has it left unsettled about the axioms? Where does the responsibility for choosing those axioms remain?"}, {"title": "Hilbert’s program and consistency", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-1-2", "text": "1.1.2 Hilbert’s program and consistency § Writing rules explicitly lets us check whether an argument follows them. But are the rules themselves reliable? Could they allow us to prove both a statement and its opposite? A system that never does this is called consistent . Hilbert wanted to put mathematics on a secure foundation. First, its assumptions and proof rules would be made explicit. Then one would show that those rules could never produce a contradiction. He hoped to give this justification using reasoning about finite objects and concrete operations on them. These were called finitary methods . Even when a mathematical statement concerns infinitely many numbers, its written proof is finite. So, proofs and the rules for constructing them could themselves be studied. This was a question about the reliability of mathematical reasoning. A related question concerns its automation: once the rules are explicit, can a procedure always determine whether a statement has a proof? To see the difficulty, we must distinguish checking a proof from searching for one. Hilbert’s aim was to study the finite proofs allowed by a system and show that none could lead to a contradiction. Problems What does it mean for a formal system to be consistent? A system proves \\(P\\) and also proves “not \\(P\\).” Which requirement of Hilbert’s program has failed? Suppose we justify one system of rules by reasoning in another. What must we then trust about the second system? Why did Hilbert seek a particularly restricted form of reasoning for this justification?"}, {"title": "Proof checking, proof search, and decision", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-1-3", "text": "1.1.3 Proof checking, proof search, and decision § Suppose we are given a proposed proof. We can check its steps and accept or reject that particular argument. Rejection does not show that the conclusion has no proof; a different argument might work. What if no proof is supplied? Suppose proofs use a fixed, finite collection of symbols. We can list candidate strings in order of length and check each one. Most will not be proofs. But every finite proof eventually appears, so this search finds a proof whenever one exists. What happens if there is no proof? The search keeps trying longer candidates. It never reaches a last candidate that certifies failure. A decision procedure must do more. For every statement we give it, it must finish with a correct answer: “a proof exists” or “no proof exists.” Our search method does not meet that requirement. Could another method succeed? Failure of one search strategy does not show that every possible strategy fails. To investigate that question, we need to specify what counts as a mechanical procedure. This leads us to models of computation . Checking tests one proposed proof. Searching tries candidates until it finds a proof. Deciding must also finish when no proof exists. Problems Does checking a supplied proof require finding that proof first? A checker rejects one proposed proof. Explain why this does not show that the statement has no proof. Imagine searching simultaneously for a proof of a statement and a proof of its negation. Under what assumptions would this become a decision procedure? What happens if neither proof exists?"}, {"title": "Algorithms and termination", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-2-1", "text": "1.2.1 Algorithms and termination § Let us first consider a procedure whose steps we can examine directly. We want the greatest common divisor of two positive integers: the largest positive integer that divides both without remainder. Euclid’s algorithm repeatedly divides and keeps the remainder. For \\(48\\) and \\(18\\), we obtain \\[48=2\\cdot18+12,\\qquad18=1\\cdot12+6,\\qquad12=2\\cdot6+0.\\] The pairs change as follows: \\[(48,18)\\longrightarrow(18,12)\\longrightarrow(12,6)\\longrightarrow(6,0).\\] At a step with pair \\((a,b)\\), suppose \\(b\\) is positive. Divide \\(a\\) by \\(b\\). Write the result as \\(a=qb+r\\), where \\(q\\) is the quotient and \\(r\\) is the remainder. The remainder satisfies \\(0\\le r\\lt b\\). Replace \\((a,b)\\) by \\((b,r)\\) and repeat. When the second entry becomes zero, return the first. In our example, we reach \\((6,0)\\), so the answer is six. Why does this give the correct answer? Suppose a number divides both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). It must also divide \\(a-qb\\), which is the remainder \\(r\\). So, every common divisor of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is a common divisor of \\(b\\) and \\(r\\). The reverse also holds. A number dividing both \\(b\\) and \\(r\\) divides \\(qb+r=a\\). Thus the old pair and the new pair have exactly the same common divisors. In particular, their greatest common divisor is unchanged. A property that remains unchanged at every step is called an invariant . Why does the procedure finish? Each new second entry is smaller than the previous one, and it is never negative. A sequence of nonnegative integers cannot keep decreasing forever. Eventually the second entry is zero. At that point the pair is \\((g,0)\\). Its greatest common divisor is \\(g\\): every divisor of \\(g\\) also divides zero. Since the greatest common divisor has stayed unchanged, \\(g\\) is the answer for the original pair. We have established two things: the steps preserve the answer, and the process reaches its stopping condition. These require separate arguments. A procedure that repeats the same pair forever preserves the greatest common divisor but never returns it. So, preserving the answer alone is not enough. The greatest common divisor remains six throughout this run. The second entry decreases through nonnegative integers until it is zero. These are separate reasons for correctness and termination. Problems When does Euclid’s algorithm stop? Starting from (18,12), write the next two pairs and give the greatest common divisor. Euclid’s algorithm preserves the answer while decreasing the second entry. Why are both facts needed? Can you describe a procedure that preserves the right answer at every step but never reaches it?"}, {"title": "Configurations and transitions", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-2-2", "text": "1.2.2 Configurations and transitions § Suppose we pause Euclid’s algorithm at the pair \\((18,12)\\), just before the next division. What must we record in order to continue? We need the pair and the instruction that comes next. We do not need the earlier pairs. The information needed to resume a computation is called its configuration . What must we include? That depends on where we allow the computation to pause. At the start of Euclid’s repeated step, we need only the pair: the next instruction is already fixed. Halfway through a division, the pair alone would not be enough. We would also need the temporary values and a record of how far the division had progressed. A transition is one step from a configuration to the next. Starting with a particular input, we obtain a sequence of configurations. This sequence is called a run . The algorithm supplies the transition rules and tells us when to stop. Proof search can be described in the same way. We record the candidate being checked, the line currently under examination, and any other information needed by the checker. Each operation updates this configuration. For a deterministic procedure, the current configuration determines the next step. Does it also determine the previous step? In Euclid’s algorithm, both \\((30,18)\\) and \\((48,18)\\) lead to \\((18,12)\\). So, knowing how to continue does not necessarily tell us how to go backward. We will return to this distinction when we consider reversibility. A configuration includes enough information to resume the computation. A transition updates that information; a run is a sequence of these updates. Problems Name the three parts of a Turing-machine configuration. Two machines have the same tape and control state but different head positions. Explain why their next steps could differ. Two executions have the same control state but different tape contents. Must their futures agree? What information must a configuration include before it really determines the next step?"}, {"title": "Turing machines and universal computation", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-2-3", "text": "1.2.3 Turing machines and universal computation § We have described computation as a sequence of transitions. But an instruction such as “divide” still contains many operations. To study computation in general, we need a model with explicitly specified elementary steps. A Turing machine has a tape divided into cells. Each cell holds a symbol from a fixed, finite collection, including a blank symbol. A head reads one cell at a time. The machine also has an internal state chosen from a finite list. The state records which stage of its instructions it is in. A rule table uses two pieces of information: the current state and the symbol under the head. For each relevant pair, the rule tells the machine what to write, whether to move one cell left or right, and which state to enter next. If it enters the halting state, the computation stops. For example, place a finite block of \\(1\\) symbols on an otherwise blank tape, with the head at the left end. Give the machine the rule to move right whenever it reads \\(1\\), and to halt when it reads a blank. The same finite instructions work for a block of any finite length. To resume this machine, we need the tape contents, the head position, and the internal state. Together, these make up its configuration. The model does not fix a maximum tape length in advance. More cells are available when needed. This does not mean that a completed calculation uses infinite memory: after finitely many steps, the head has visited only finitely many cells. With more rules, the machine can carry out arithmetic or check proofs. Its own rule table is also a finite object, so we can encode that table as a string of symbols. Another machine can read this description and imitate the described machine, one step at a time. A universal Turing machine can do this for any correctly encoded machine and input. Changing the description changes the computation it simulates. Now a program can be treated as data. We can supply its description to another program, or even supply it to itself. This requires only copying a finite string. It will be useful when we ask what programs can determine about other programs. This gives a general model of computation. We can use it to describe particular algorithms and to formulate questions about all machines in the model. The highlighted cell is under the head. In this example, the machine moves right over each 1 and halts at a blank. A universal machine uses encoded instructions to simulate another machine; it need not halt if the simulated run does not halt. Problems What does a universal Turing machine simulate? To simulate a particular program on a particular input, what two pieces of information must the universal machine receive? A universal machine treats another machine’s instructions as input. What does this change about the boundary between a program and its data? Does the ability to simulate every machine imply the ability to predict which simulations finish?"}, {"title": "The Church–Turing thesis", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-3-1", "text": "1.3.1 The Church–Turing thesis § We have defined a model using a tape, a head, and a finite rule table. Why use this model to discuss the limits of effective calculation? Could another reasonable model compute functions that Turing machines cannot? Other mathematical models describe computation in different ways. Lambda calculus uses rules for substituting expressions. Recursive-function models build functions from specified basic functions and construction rules. Despite these differences, they compute the same functions as Turing machines. A function computable in one model is computable in the others. This equivalence is a mathematical result that can be proved. The Church–Turing thesis makes a further claim: any calculation that can be carried out by an effective mechanical method can be carried out by a Turing machine. The phrase “effective mechanical method” is an informal idea. A Turing machine is a precisely defined object. The thesis connects the two. The agreement between different formal models supports it, but does not turn it into a theorem about an already defined class of every possible mechanical method. This distinction matters when we establish a limitation. We can prove that no Turing machine solves a specified problem. Applying that result to effective procedures in general uses the thesis. The thesis concerns whether a function can be computed at all. It does not say how much time or memory the computation needs. This matters for quantum computing. In the standard quantum model, the allowed operations must themselves have effective mathematical descriptions. Under this condition, quantum computers do not compute functions that are uncomputable by Turing machines. They can change the resources needed for some tasks that are already computable. We will return to this difference when studying quantum algorithms. The solid arrows connect formal models that can simulate one another. They compute the same functions, though they may take different amounts of time. The dashed arrow marks the further claim of the Church–Turing thesis: these models capture what we mean informally by an effective method. Problems Which informal idea does the Church–Turing thesis connect to Turing machines? A new programming language can be simulated by a Turing machine. Does this establish that it computes more functions than a Turing machine? Explain. Why does agreement between several formal models support the Church–Turing thesis without proving it in the same way as a theorem about those models? What would a proposed counterexample have to explain?"}, {"title": "The halting problem", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-3-2", "text": "1.3.2 The halting problem § Given a program and its input, can we always determine whether the computation will stop? We know that Euclid’s algorithm stops for its permitted inputs. We also know that a program instructed to repeat forever does not stop. Can one procedure settle this question for every program and input? Suppose such a procedure exists. Call it \\(H(P,x)\\), where \\(P\\) is a program description and \\(x\\) is its input. We require \\(H\\) itself to finish and answer correctly on every valid pair. Let us use \\(H\\) to construct another program, \\(D\\). Its input \\(p\\) is a program description. First, \\(D\\) calls \\(H(p,p)\\): it asks what happens when the program described by \\(p\\) receives its own description as input. Then \\(D\\) takes the opposite action. If \\(H\\) answers “halts,” \\(D\\) enters an endless loop. If \\(H\\) answers “does not halt,” \\(D\\) stops. If the input is not a valid program description, we can simply let \\(D\\) stop. Now let \\(d\\) be the description of \\(D\\), and give \\(d\\) to \\(D\\). What should \\(H(d,d)\\) answer? If it answers “halts,” the instructions of \\(D\\) make this run continue forever. If it answers “does not halt,” the instructions make the run stop. In either case, the prediction is wrong. We assumed that \\(H\\) was an algorithm that always finished and answered correctly. That assumption allowed us to use it as part of \\(D\\). But the resulting program contradicts the assumption. Therefore no such general \\(H\\) exists. This result is called the undecidability of the halting problem . This does not prevent us from determining the behavior of particular programs. We already proved that Euclid’s algorithm terminates. It also permits an analyzer that sometimes answers “unknown.” What is excluded is one algorithm that always finishes with a correct definite answer for every case. Assume \\(H\\) always finishes and predicts correctly. Program \\(D\\) follows its prediction and takes the opposite action. Now give \\(D\\) its own description \\(d\\). \\(H(d,d)\\) is predicting this very run, so either answer is contradicted by what \\(D\\) does. Problems What two possible answers must a halting decider give? A program immediately stops. Another repeats an instruction forever. Can you decide these two cases without solving the general halting problem? The diagonal program behaves against the halting predictor when given its own description. Why does this produce a contradiction for a supposedly universal predictor, but not for a tool that correctly analyzes only some programs?"}, {"title": "Undecidability and incompleteness", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-3-3", "text": "1.3.3 Undecidability and incompleteness § We began by asking whether explicit rules could make mathematical reasoning reliable and whether a procedure could settle every question about proofs. The halting result shows how to prove a limitation on procedures. We now briefly consider the corresponding limits on what a system of axioms can prove. A problem is undecidable if no algorithm always finishes with the correct yes-or-no answer for every input. A theory is incomplete if some sentence can neither be proved nor refuted using its axioms and rules. The first is a limitation on procedures; the second is a limitation on what follows from chosen assumptions. To state the Gödel–Rosser incompleteness theorem, first consider a theory with three properties. It is consistent. Its axioms can be listed by an algorithm. And it contains enough arithmetic to express basic calculations with nonnegative integers. For a concrete example of the last requirement, take Peano arithmetic. This is the usual first-order theory of nonnegative integers with addition, multiplication, and induction. The theorem applies to consistent theories that include these axioms and whose axioms can be listed algorithmically. It also applies to certain weaker arithmetic theories. The conclusion is that some sentence \\(P\\) has neither a proof nor a refutation in the theory. The theory proves neither \\(P\\) nor \\(\\neg P\\). A theory whose axioms can be listed by an algorithm is called effectively axiomatized . The list may be infinite: each axiom only needs to appear after finitely many steps. A proof can include a record showing when a cited axiom appears on that list. This lets a checker verify the use of that axiom in finite time. Can we settle an unanswered sentence by adding it as a new axiom? We can. But if the enlarged theory still meets the theorem’s conditions, some sentence will again remain unsettled. It need not be the same sentence. The result does not apply to every formal theory; the conditions matter. Gödel’s second incompleteness theorem concerns the consistency question. A theory with enough arithmetic can write statements about its own encoded proofs. In particular, it can express the claim that none of those proofs derives a contradiction. For theories satisfying the theorem’s arithmetic and proof-representation conditions, the result is this: if the theory is consistent, it cannot prove that standard statement of its own consistency. So, a consistency argument whose steps can all be carried out within the same theory cannot provide this justification. A stronger theory may prove that a weaker one is consistent. But we must then examine the assumptions of the stronger theory. So, formal reasoning has precise limitations. We can still prove particular statements and construct algorithms for particular tasks. For the tasks that admit algorithms, we can now ask how those algorithms should be implemented. On the left, we ask whether an algorithm can decide a problem for every input. On the right, we ask what a particular theory can prove. The incompleteness result requires the stated conditions on that theory. An unsuccessful search alone establishes neither result. Problems Does “unprovable in this theory” mean “disproved in this theory”? A sentence has neither a proof nor a disproof in a theory. State what would change if it were added as an axiom. If a sentence is undecidable in one formal theory, could adding it as an axiom settle it there? Why would that not establish a general algorithm for deciding every sentence?"}, {"title": "Deterministic and reversible transitions", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-4-1", "text": "1.4.1 Deterministic and reversible transitions § In a deterministic computation, each configuration has at most one next configuration. A halting configuration may have none. When the computation can continue, the current configuration fixes what happens next. Does knowing the later configuration also tell us which earlier one produced it? Determinism does not require this. For example, Euclid’s algorithm includes the transitions \\[(30,18)\\longrightarrow(18,12),\\qquad(48,18)\\longrightarrow(18,12).\\] Knowing the pair \\((18,12)\\) lets us continue the calculation, but it does not identify which earlier pair occurred. Different configurations have merged into the same one. To undo a step, we must be able to recover the earlier configuration from the full resulting configuration. This requires different inputs to have different outputs. Such a transition is called one-to-one . Logical reversibility requires this extra condition. Suppose the transition also reaches every configuration in a fixed state space. It is then a bijection : each configuration has exactly one predecessor. We can define the reverse transformation everywhere on that space. So, knowing what happens next does not necessarily tell us what happened before. We now ask whether useful computations can preserve enough information to be reversed. On the left, each input has a unique successor, but \\(C\\) has two possible predecessors. On the right, each output identifies one predecessor. Reversibility imposes a condition on the backward direction as well. Problems What does a deterministic transition rule specify? Draw \\(A\\to C\\) and \\(B\\to C\\). Is this rule deterministic on \\(A\\) and \\(B\\)? Can \\(C\\) identify its predecessor? A deterministic rule fixes one future for each present state. Why need it not fix one past? Draw a transition pattern that separates these two requirements."}, {"title": "Information loss in computation", "chapter": "Logic and Computability", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#topic-1-4-2", "text": "1.4.2 Information loss in computation § Consider the Boolean AND operation. Its output is one only when both input bits are one. The inputs \\(00\\), \\(01\\), and \\(10\\) all produce zero. If we retain only this output, we cannot recover the input pair. Must we discard the input to obtain the answer? Suppose we want to calculate \\(f(x)\\). If we replace \\(x\\) by its answer, two different inputs may become indistinguishable. If we keep both the input and the answer as \\((x,f(x))\\), the input remains available. This suggests using extra storage while carrying out the calculation. Keeping the input is only a starting idea. We must choose elementary steps that can be undone. We must say what values the extra storage holds at the start. We must also decide what to do with temporary results. The next chapter develops these constructions using Boolean functions and circuits. Why does this matter for quantum computing? When a quantum system evolves without interacting with anything outside it, its evolution is described by a unitary transformation . Such a transformation has an inverse: mathematically, it can be undone. This is why reversible operations are important in quantum circuits. We need to learn how to obtain an answer through steps that preserve this possibility of reversal. Measurement and discarding part of a system are different kinds of operations, which we will treat separately. We have moved from asking whether a task admits an algorithm to asking how the algorithm transforms information. The limits of computability do not settle this implementation question. We now need to examine the operations used in the computation itself. On the left, the AND output does not tell us which input produced it. On the right, we keep both input bits and put their AND in an extra target bit, initially zero. The full update uses XOR, or addition modulo two. Applying it twice restores the original three bits. Chapter 2 explains the construction. Problems What information is lost when both 0 and 1 are reset to 0? Compare returning only \\(a+b\\) with returning \\((a,b,a+b)\\). Which output allows you to recover both inputs? If two inputs lead to the same answer, has the computation necessarily erased information? How does your answer depend on whether you inspect only the output or the entire machine state?"}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-1", "text": "A proof search has not found a proof after one million candidates. Does this establish unprovability? No. It establishes only that the checked records were not proofs of the sentence. An independent argument would be needed to establish unprovability."}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-2", "text": "Run Euclid’s algorithm on (84, 30). Explain separately why the answer is correct and why the run stops. The pairs are \\((84,30)\\to(30,24)\\to(24,6)\\to(6,0)\\), so the answer is 6. Replacing \\((a,b)\\) with \\((b,a-qb)\\) preserves common divisors. The nonnegative second entry strictly decreases until it reaches zero. These justify correctness and termination separately."}, {"title": "Logic and Computability — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "01-logic-computability.html#exercise-3", "text": "Give two distinct predecessors of (7, 3) under one Euclid step. What information would distinguish them? \\((10,7)\\) and \\((17,7)\\) both become \\((7,3)\\). Retaining the quotient \\(q\\) distinguishes these predecessors: \\(a=7q+3\\). More generally, a reversible implementation must preserve enough information to reconstruct each previous full state."}, {"title": "Binary encodings", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-1-1", "text": "2.1.1 Binary encodings § A finite input can be represented by bits once an encoding is chosen. An integer may use binary digits; a string may encode characters; a graph may list edges. Encoding determines input length, which later affects resource counts. At a fixed length \\(n\\), there are \\(2^n\\) possible inputs. If outputs have a fixed bound, they too can be encoded, with padding if necessary. We first study a function on this finite domain. A procedure handling arbitrary lengths requires a systematic family of such functions, not a separate unrelated construction for every length. An encoding supplies meaning. The bits alone do not tell a machine whether to treat \\(01000001\\) as a number or a character. Problems How many different strings can two bits represent? Write the integers zero through three using two-bit binary strings. The same bit string can represent an integer, a character, or an instruction. Which part of its meaning belongs to the bits, and which part belongs to the encoding convention? How could changing the encoding change the cost of a task?"}, {"title": "Boolean functions and truth tables", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-1-2", "text": "2.1.2 Boolean functions and truth tables § A Boolean function \\(f:\\{0,1\\}^n\\to\\{0,1\\}\\) specifies a yes/no answer for each bit string. Its truth table lists all inputs and their outputs. For OR on two bits, the outputs for \\(00,01,10,11\\) are \\(0,1,1,1\\). A multi-bit answer is a collection of Boolean output functions. This description specifies what to compute but not how. It hides neither an algorithm nor an efficiency guarantee: writing the full table already takes exponentially many rows in \\(n\\). We need a way to turn the specification into operations. The truth table lists four possible inputs. Only the input \\(11\\) produces one; a circuit must implement all four rows. Problems What values can a Boolean variable take? List all input pairs for a two-input AND gate and give their outputs. A truth table specifies every output of a Boolean function. Why does possessing this complete description not necessarily give an efficient way to compute the function as the number of inputs grows?"}, {"title": "Polynomial representation over the binary field", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-1-3", "text": "2.1.3 Polynomial representation over the binary field § Work modulo two: addition is XOR, so \\(1+1=0\\), and multiplication is AND. For a row \\(s\\), define a detector \\[\\delta_s(x)=\\prod_{i:s_i=1}x_i\\prod_{i:s_i=0}(1+x_i).\\] It equals one exactly when \\(x=s\\). Therefore \\[f(x)=\\bigoplus_{s:f(s)=1}\\delta_s(x).\\] On any input, at most one included detector is one, so XOR gives the required answer. Expanding produces a polynomial in the input bits. For OR, the expansion simplifies to \\(x+y+xy\\). Substituting \\(x=y=1\\) gives \\(1\\), as required; ordinary integer addition would give the wrong interpretation. All additions are modulo two. The polynomial \\(x+y+xy\\) reproduces OR on Boolean inputs; its interpretation depends on this arithmetic. Problems What are \\(1+1\\) and \\(1\\times1\\) in arithmetic modulo two? Evaluate \\(x+y+xy\\) modulo two for all four bit pairs. Which Boolean operation does it describe? Why can \\(x^2\\) be replaced by \\(x\\) for Boolean inputs even though these are different polynomials over unrestricted numbers? What does this tell you about the role of the allowed input domain in an algebraic representation?"}, {"title": "Polynomial evaluation by circuits", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-2-1", "text": "2.2.1 Polynomial evaluation by circuits § A circuit evaluates this polynomial by connecting operations: multiply factors with AND, add terms with XOR, and use fixed bits for constants. Wires carry intermediate bits; a gate’s outputs become later inputs. The circuit has no directed cycles, so its gates can be evaluated in order. This construction proves that every fixed-size Boolean function has a circuit. It may use exponentially many gates. Universality—being able to express every function—is a different claim from being able to express every function economically. To evaluate \\(z=ab+c\\) over bits, compute the product once and pass that bit to XOR. Here \\(+\\) means XOR, so the diagram is also a circuit plan. Problems What does a wire carry between Boolean gates? For \\(z=(a \\text{ AND } b) \\text{ XOR } c\\), identify the intermediate value and draw the two operations in order. Two algebraically equivalent expressions can produce circuits of different sizes. Where can sharing an intermediate result save gates, and what extra information about a computation does a circuit reveal beyond its truth table?"}, {"title": "NAND universality", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-2-2", "text": "2.2.2 NAND universality § NAND returns \\(\\neg(a\\land b)\\). It can build the operations needed above: tying its inputs gives \\(\\neg a\\), and negating a NAND output gives AND. XOR uses four NANDs: \\[t=\\operatorname{NAND}(a,b),\\quad u=\\operatorname{NAND}(a,t),\\quad v=\\operatorname{NAND}(b,t),\\quad a\\oplus b=\\operatorname{NAND}(u,v).\\] Checking the four input pairs verifies the last identity. Since NAND constructs AND, XOR, and negation, it evaluates the polynomial for any Boolean function. Classical fan-out is allowed here: the same bit may feed several gates. This is not yet a reversible construction. Four NAND gates compute XOR. Branch dots indicate shared classical inputs; small output circles indicate negation. Problems When does NAND output zero? Use \\(\\mathrm{NAND}(x,x)\\) to compute the output for \\(x=0\\) and \\(x=1\\). Which operation have you built? NAND can express every Boolean function. Does that make it the best gate for every implementation? Separate the claim that something can be built from the claim that it can be built economically."}, {"title": "Irreversible Boolean operations", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-2-3", "text": "2.2.3 Irreversible Boolean operations § An AND output of zero could have come from \\(00\\), \\(01\\), or \\(10\\). If only the output remains, those inputs cannot be distinguished. The map is many-to-one and has no inverse. This is logical information loss, regardless of whether the gate was useful. A complicated circuit may implement a one-to-one overall input–output function while erasing internal records along the way. An overall reversible truth table does not by itself establish that each physical step is reversible or avoids dissipation. The AND gate maps \\(00\\), \\(01\\), and \\(10\\) to the same output. Output zero cannot identify which of these inputs occurred. Problems Can you always recover both AND inputs from its output? List the input pairs that give AND output zero. Use the list to explain the loss of information. An AND output of one identifies its input, while an output of zero does not. Why is the gate still classified as irreversible? Is reversibility a property of one observed run or of the whole allowed map?"}, {"title": "Landauer’s principle", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-3-1", "text": "2.3.1 Landauer’s principle § A bit lives in a physical system. Resetting an unknown bit to zero merges two logical possibilities. Landauer’s principle assigns a minimum average heat cost \\(k_{\\mathrm B}T\\ln2\\) to erasing one unbiased bit in the usual thermal setting, without retaining a compensating record. Here \\(T\\) is temperature and \\(k_{\\mathrm B}\\) is Boltzmann’s constant. The conditions matter: known bits, biased distributions, and available correlations change the information that must be erased. The principle is not a fixed energy charge on every logical gate. Its role here is to connect lost information to physical resources. Further detail: Bennett: Landauer’s principle and reversible computation . Both possible logical inputs are reset to zero. For erasure of one equally likely bit in the standard isothermal setting, the minimum heat delivered to the environment is kBT ln 2. Problems Under the stated conditions, what happens to Landauer’s minimum erasure cost when temperature increases? Compare erasing two independent, equally likely bits with erasing one at the same temperature. How do their minimum costs compare? Erasing a known zero and erasing an unknown bit can leave the same final register value. What differs about the information being discarded? Why must a physical claim about erasure specify the initial situation?"}, {"title": "Logical reversibility and dissipation", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-3-2", "text": "2.3.2 Logical reversibility and dissipation § A logically reversible map is one-to-one on its allowed states. It avoids the necessity of erasure at the level of that map, but a real implementation can still dissipate energy through resistance, friction, control, and imperfect operation. Conversely, an irreversible gate cannot be made logically invertible merely by operating it slowly. We must distinguish the mathematical map from its physical realization. Quantum unitary evolution will supply another reason for preserving information: it has an inverse. Measurement and interaction with an environment require a different description. A reversible truth table settles whether the input is recoverable. It does not specify the energy dissipated by a particular physical device. Problems Does logical reversibility alone guarantee that a physical device produces no heat? A gate’s truth table is reversible, but its implementation dissipates energy. Explain why these statements do not contradict each other. Could a logically reversible circuit still dissipate substantial energy in its physical implementation? What does knowing its truth table tell you, and what physical details does that table leave out?"}, {"title": "Reversible function embeddings", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-3-3", "text": "2.3.3 Reversible function embeddings § Any Boolean function can be embedded in a reversible operation by retaining its input: \\[U_f:(x,y)\\mapsto(x,y\\oplus f(x)).\\] Applying it twice restores \\(y\\), because XOR with the same bit twice cancels. With \\(y=0\\), the second register becomes the answer. Reversibility does not require \\(f\\) itself to be injective: the retained \\(x\\) distinguishes the original inputs. Extra bits are therefore not incidental bookkeeping. They are part of the state needed to make the full transformation one-to-one. For \\(f(x)=1\\), the target flips. Applying the same reversible oracle twice restores the input and target because XOR by the same bit cancels. Problems Which register is preserved in \\((x,t)\\to(x,t \\text{ XOR } f(x))\\)? Set \\(f(0)=1\\) and start with \\((x,t)=(0,0)\\). Apply the update twice. What returns to its original value? A reversible embedding retains the input while updating a target bit. Has it made the original many-to-one function invertible, or changed what counts as the full output? Explain why this distinction matters."}, {"title": "Controlled reversible operations", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-4-1", "text": "2.4.1 Controlled reversible operations § A controlled operation retains a control value while reversibly updating a target. CNOT maps \\((a,b)\\) to \\((a,b\\oplus a)\\). When \\(a=0\\), nothing changes; when \\(a=1\\), the target flips. In either case, the same operation undoes the change. Control is not synonymous with reversibility: the target operation must itself be reversible for every retained control value. This example introduces a useful design method—choose among invertible updates without discarding which update was chosen. CNOT preserves its control bit. For control zero it leaves the target alone; for control one it flips the target. Each branch can be undone. Problems In a controlled-NOT gate, which bit determines whether the target flips? Apply CNOT to \\(00\\), \\(01\\), \\(10\\), and \\(11\\), taking the first bit as control. A controlled gate uses one bit to determine what happens to another while preserving the control. Why is preserving the control helpful when reconstructing the past? Would merely knowing that a conditional instruction was executed be enough?"}, {"title": "CNOT, Toffoli, and Fredkin gates", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-4-2", "text": "2.4.2 CNOT, Toffoli, and Fredkin gates § Toffoli maps \\((a,b,c)\\) to \\((a,b,c\\oplus ab)\\). A zero target records AND while preserving its inputs. Fredkin retains a control and swaps two targets when that control is one. Both are their own inverses. Toffoli with initialized workspace can implement general reversible Boolean computations; CNOT alone cannot produce the nonlinear term \\(ab\\). In circuit drawings a filled dot denotes a control on one, and a circled plus denotes an XOR target. A vertical connector means these symbols belong to one joint gate. CNOT, Toffoli, and Fredkin preserve their full inputs in reversible form. Each drawing shows one gate, with time running left to right. Problems How many control bits does a Toffoli gate have? Apply Toffoli to \\(110\\) and \\(100\\), with the last bit as target. Explain why only one target flips. CNOT and Toffoli are both reversible, but Toffoli can write the AND of two inputs into a blank target. What additional dependence on the inputs does Toffoli introduce? Why does reversibility alone not determine a gate’s computational usefulness?"}, {"title": "Workspace and uncomputation", "chapter": "Boolean and Reversible Computation", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#topic-2-4-3", "text": "2.4.3 Workspace and uncomputation § A reversible calculation often yields \\((x,f(x),g(x))\\), where \\(g(x)\\) is temporary “garbage.” Resetting it would erase information. Instead, compute, XOR the result into a clean output register, and run the computation backward: \\[(x,0,0)\\to(x,f(x),g(x))\\to(x,f(x),g(x);f(x))\\to(x,0,0;f(x)).\\] The notation groups workspace schematically; only the copied answer remains in the fresh register. In a quantum computation this copies basis labels coherently, not an arbitrary unknown state. Uncomputation becomes essential when unwanted workspace records would obstruct interference . Compute the answer, copy its basis label to a fresh output, then apply the inverse computation. A may act on a larger workspace than the two schematic wires shown. Problems What is temporary workspace used for? Put these steps in order: reverse the computation, compute the result, copy the basis-state result into a clean output. If a computation retains every intermediate value, it can preserve its history but exhaust its workspace. How does computing, recording the result, and running backward avoid keeping that history forever? What information remains at the end?"}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-1", "text": "Is AND reversible if only its one-bit output is retained? No. The output does not uniquely identify the input. Keeping the inputs changes the full map and allows a reversible embedding."}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-2", "text": "Apply \\((x,y,t)\\mapsto(x,y,t \\text{ XOR } xy)\\) to \\(110\\), \\(111\\), and \\(010\\). Then apply it again. \\(110\\to111\\to110\\); \\(111\\to110\\to111\\); \\(010\\to010\\to010\\). The gate is its own inverse on all three bits, even though AND alone is many-to-one."}, {"title": "Boolean and Reversible Computation — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "02-boolean-reversible-computation.html#exercise-3", "text": "After computing an answer and temporary workspace, why not simply reset the workspace to zero? Describe a reversible alternative for classical basis inputs. Resetting erases the distinctions stored in the workspace. Compute the result, XOR it into a clean output register, then apply the inverse computation to the original registers. This restores the workspace while retaining the input and recorded output. Copying basis labels this way does not clone an arbitrary unknown quantum state."}, {"title": "Outcomes, distributions, and frequencies", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-1", "text": "3.1.1 Outcomes, distributions, and frequencies § A classical bit has a value zero or one. If its preparation is uncertain, describe that uncertainty by probabilities \\(p_0,p_1\\geq0\\) with \\(p_0+p_1=1\\). Repeating the same preparation independently gives outcome frequencies that tend toward these probabilities; a short sample need not match them exactly. The distribution describes uncertainty across outcomes, not a fraction of a bit observed in one trial. This distinction between a predictive description and an individual result remains important in quantum mechanics. The bar heights are probabilities, not guaranteed frequencies in a short sample. Their sum is one. Problems What must the probabilities of all possible outcomes sum to? A device produces 0 with probability 0.7. What is the probability of 1 if these are its only outcomes? A fair coin can produce ten heads in a row. What would such a record tell you about the claim that the coin is fair? Why can a probability model be tested by frequencies without requiring every finite sample to match its probabilities?"}, {"title": "Conditional probability and path weights", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-2", "text": "3.1.2 Conditional probability and path weights § Suppose a transition has conditional probabilities \\(P(j\\mid i)\\): the probability of next state \\(j\\) given current state \\(i\\). The probability of a two-step path \\(i\\to j\\to k\\) is the product of its transition probabilities. To obtain the probability of reaching \\(k\\), sum over the disjoint intermediate alternatives: \\[P(k\\mid i)=\\sum_j P(k\\mid j)P(j\\mid i),\\] when the transition rule depends only on the current state. These are the product and sum rules for a Markov process. A fair randomizing step has every matrix entry \\(1/2\\); applying it twice still leaves a fair distribution. First choose the left branch with probability \\(1/2\\). Conditional on that branch, success has probability \\(1/3\\). The probability of this complete route is \\(1/6\\). Problems How do you calculate the probability of a route with two successive conditional steps? A route takes its first step with probability \\(1/2\\) and its second with conditional probability \\(1/3\\). Calculate its probability. Two different routes lead to the same final outcome. When is adding their route probabilities justified? What assumptions about the routes and their conditional weights are hidden in that calculation?"}, {"title": "Nondeterministic and probabilistic transitions", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-1-3", "text": "3.1.3 Nondeterministic and probabilistic transitions § A nondeterministic model records permitted successors, not likelihoods. A computation accepts if some permitted branch accepts. Branch weights can be written as zero or one, but their combination uses logical AND along a path and OR across paths. Two successful paths still mean “possible,” not probability two. A probabilistic model instead weights branches and asks how likely acceptance is. Keeping these meanings distinct prevents a common confusion: quantum amplitudes are neither a list of permitted classical guesses nor ordinary probabilities. With eight equally likely paths and one successful path, nondeterministic acceptance and randomized success answer different questions. Problems Does a nondeterministic choice specify probabilities? A random procedure chooses uniformly among eight paths, one successful. Find its success probability and compare it with the statement “a successful path exists.” A nondeterministic description says that a successful path exists. A probabilistic procedure must actually reach one. Why does the first statement alone give no guarantee of a useful success probability?"}, {"title": "A two-state quantum transformation", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-2-1", "text": "3.2.1 A two-state quantum transformation § Consider the Hadamard transformation \\[H=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] For a definite input zero, it produces amplitudes \\((1/\\sqrt2,1/\\sqrt2)^T\\); input one gives \\((1/\\sqrt2,-1/\\sqrt2)^T\\). Measuring the output in the zero–one basis gives each outcome with probability one half, the squared magnitude of either entry. For now take these as the predictions of a quantum operation. The negative sign is invisible in this single measurement. The next calculation shows why it cannot be discarded from the state description. Starting in \\(\\ket0\\), \\(H\\) produces equal amplitudes. The computational-basis measurement therefore has two equally likely outcomes. Problems What state does \\(H\\) produce from \\(\\ket0\\)? Use \\(H\\ket0=(\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) to calculate the probabilities of measuring 0 and 1. One use of a Hadamard gate gives equal outcome probabilities from either basis input. If you retained only those probabilities, what prediction would you make for two uses? Which information would your description have lost?"}, {"title": "Composition of transitions", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-2-2", "text": "3.2.2 Composition of transitions § Matrix multiplication gives \\(H^2=I\\). Starting at zero, two successive Hadamards return zero with certainty. The fair randomizing classical step does not: its square is still a fair randomizer. The comparison assumes no measurement between the quantum steps. Inserting a measurement after the first Hadamard destroys the coherence needed for the certain return. We are not claiming that all classical devices lack hidden internal information; rather, a model that retains only the two outcome probabilities cannot reproduce this coherent composition. No measurement occurs between the two Hadamards. Their product is the identity. Problems Which transformation acts first in the matrix product BA applied to a column vector? Apply \\(H\\) to \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) using the two basis-state rules. Combine the coefficients of each basis state. Why does composing two quantum transformations require their amplitudes rather than just their one-step outcome probabilities? Use the two-Hadamard example to locate exactly where a probability-only calculation fails."}, {"title": "Signed amplitudes and outcome probabilities", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-2-3", "text": "3.2.3 Signed amplitudes and outcome probabilities § Amplitudes combine before they are converted to probabilities. Two paths contributing \\(+1/2\\) and \\(-1/2\\) to the same final outcome sum to zero, hence give zero probability. Squaring each first would instead give \\(1/4+1/4=1/2\\), a different prediction. The order of operations matters: \\[p(k)=\\left|\\sum_{\\text{paths to }k}\\text{path amplitude}\\right|^2.\\] This rule applies to alternatives that remain coherent. If a measurement or environment keeps a distinguishable record of the alternatives, we must include that record in the joint description rather than summing as if it were absent. Squaring magnitudes removes a sign. The sign can still affect later interference when an amplitude is added to another contribution. Problems How do you obtain probability from an amplitude? Calculate the probabilities associated with amplitudes \\(1/2\\) and \\(-1/2\\). Are they different? Both \\(+a\\) and \\(-a\\) give the same probability when considered alone. In what sense can their difference still be physically meaningful? What additional operation would allow the difference to affect an observation?"}, {"title": "Sum and product rules", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-3-1", "text": "3.3.1 Sum and product rules § For two Hadamards with input zero, the path through intermediate zero contributes \\(1/2\\) to final zero, and the path through intermediate one contributes another \\(1/2\\). The final amplitude is one. For final one, the contributions are \\(1/2\\) and \\(-1/2\\), summing to zero. Thus both outcomes are accounted for, and the probabilities sum to one. This is matrix multiplication read as a path calculation: \\((H^2)_{k0}=\\sum_j H_{kj}H_{j0}\\). The intermediate label is a bookkeeping basis, not necessarily an observed trajectory. Multiply along each route, then add contributions to the same final label. Paths through the two intermediate labels are coherent alternatives. Problems Do amplitudes add or multiply along successive steps of one route? Two coherent routes contribute \\(1/2\\) and \\(1/2\\) to the same outcome. Add them and calculate the resulting probability. Why do amplitudes multiply along a route but add across coherent routes? Trace two routes to one output and identify the point where taking squared magnitudes too early would change the prediction."}, {"title": "Constructive and destructive interference", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-3-2", "text": "3.3.2 Constructive and destructive interference § Constructive interference means contributions reinforce; destructive interference means they partly or completely cancel. Neither creates probability from nothing. A unitary transformation redistributes amplitudes while preserving their total squared magnitude. An algorithm must arrange reinforcement at useful outcomes and cancellation elsewhere. Merely creating many nonzero amplitudes does not accomplish this. The two-Hadamard example is small enough that every term can be inspected, but the same sum-of-products structure governs a register with many basis labels. Each row describes contributions to a single outcome. A minus sign changes interference, not the requirement that a probability be nonnegative. Problems What is destructive interference? Two coherent contributions to an outcome are \\(1/2\\) and \\(-1/2\\). Calculate their total amplitude and the outcome probability. If adding a second route makes an outcome less likely, which assumption behind the classical picture of mutually exclusive routes has failed? Does destructive interference mean that a negative probability was ever present?"}, {"title": "Path information and interference", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-3-3", "text": "3.3.3 Path information and interference § Write a state vector as a ket, \\(\\ket r\\); the overlap of two vectors is written \\(\\braket{r_0}{r_1}\\). If the two intermediate alternatives leave orthogonal records \\(\\ket{r_0}\\) and \\(\\ket{r_1}\\), the final amplitude contributions are attached to different joint states. Their squared norms add rather than cancel. More generally, the interference term is proportional to the overlap \\(\\braket{r_0}{r_1}\\). Identical records preserve full interference; orthogonal records remove it from the unobserved-register statistics. Chapter 5 develops these joint states. For now the practical lesson is that information left elsewhere matters even when nobody reads it. Observation by a conscious observer is not required. For the two-Hadamard experiment, identical environment records preserve interference. Orthogonal records distinguish the intermediate paths and remove the cross term in the qubit probabilities. Problems Can a path record matter even if nobody reads it? One record is identical for both paths; another perfectly identifies the path. Which one preserves the possibility of interference between the paths? Why can a path record suppress interference even when no person reads it? Compare a record that distinguishes the paths perfectly with one that is identical for both paths."}, {"title": "Relative and global phase", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-4-1", "text": "3.4.1 Relative and global phase § A sign is a special case of phase. Write an amplitude as \\(re^{i\\phi}\\), where \\(r\\geq0\\) is its magnitude. Its outcome probability is \\(r^2\\). Multiplying every amplitude by the same phase changes no prediction: it is a global phase . Changing one amplitude’s phase relative to another can alter interference. For example, \\((\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) gives equal zero–one probabilities for every \\(\\phi\\), but a subsequent Hadamard gives zero with probability \\((1+\\cos\\phi)/2\\). The first two states differ only by a global sign and have the same output probabilities. Changing only one component changes the outcome after \\(H\\). Problems What is the difference between global and relative phase? Compare \\((\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), \\(-(\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), and \\((\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Which pair differs only by global phase? A phase factor on an entire state is unobservable, yet a phase factor on one component can matter. How does the second operation change a relation within the state that the first leaves unchanged?"}, {"title": "Complex numbers and phase representation", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-4-2", "text": "3.4.2 Complex numbers and phase representation § The complex number \\(i\\) satisfies \\(i^2=-1\\). For \\(z=a+ib\\), its conjugate is \\(z^*=a-ib\\), and \\(|z|^2=z^*z=a^2+b^2\\). Complex addition combines directed contributions in a plane; multiplication multiplies magnitudes and adds phases. This makes complex numbers convenient for coherent path weights. Conjugation matters because probabilities must be real and nonnegative: squaring \\(i/\\sqrt2\\) gives \\(-1/2\\), whereas its squared magnitude is \\(1/2\\). The four arrows all have magnitude one: \\(1\\), \\(i\\), \\(-1\\), and \\(-i\\). Different directions represent different phases. A relative phase compares two such directions. Problems What is the squared magnitude of \\(i\\)? For \\((\\ket0+i\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), calculate the two computational-basis probabilities. Compare them with those of \\(\\ket+\\). What intermediate states become available when relative phase varies continuously instead of being restricted to a plus or minus sign? How could a later interference experiment distinguish those states?"}, {"title": "State vectors and transition matrices", "chapter": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#topic-3-4-3", "text": "3.4.3 State vectors and transition matrices § Choose an ordering of outcomes and collect amplitudes in a column \\(\\psi\\). Put the amplitude for \\(i\\to j\\) in matrix entry \\(U_{ji}\\): column \\(i\\) is the result of the transformation on basis input \\(i\\). Then \\(\\psi'=U\\psi\\), and successive steps give \\(VU\\psi\\), with the rightmost operation acting first. The matrix is a compact description of the same path rules. Quantum states require normalization and physical transformations require additional constraints; the next chapter states those requirements and relates coordinates to measurements. The \\(X\\) matrix swaps coordinates in the computational basis. The same rule acts on every input vector, not only the two basis vectors. Problems What do the entries of a state vector represent? Multiply the matrix \\(X\\), which swaps the two coordinates, by \\((a,b)^T\\). Write the resulting state. A state vector and a matrix depend on a choice of basis. Which predictions should remain unchanged if both are expressed consistently in a different basis? Why would changing just one give misleading results?"}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-1", "text": "Two contributions to one outcome are \\(+1/2\\) and \\(-1/2\\). What is the outcome probability? The total amplitude is zero, so the probability is zero. Adding the squared magnitudes separately would incorrectly give \\(1/2\\) for coherent alternatives."}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-2", "text": "Starting in \\(\\ket0\\), apply \\(H\\), then \\(Z\\), then \\(H\\). Compare with applying \\(H\\) twice. \\(H\\) then \\(Z\\) gives \\((\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). The final \\(H\\) gives \\(\\ket1\\). By contrast \\(H^2\\ket0=\\ket0\\). The intermediate probabilities are \\(1/2\\) and \\(1/2\\) in both cases, but the relative phase changes the final result."}, {"title": "Probability Amplitudes and Interference — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "03-amplitudes-interference.html#exercise-3", "text": "Suppose an unobserved system records which intermediate basis state occurred between two Hadamards. Must the final result remain \\(\\ket0\\)? No. After the first \\(H\\) and recording, the state is \\((\\ket0\\ket{e_0}+\\ket1\\ket{e_1})/\\sqrt2\\). After the second \\(H\\), the \\(\\ket0\\) component is \\((\\ket{e_0}+\\ket{e_1})/2\\). Its squared norm is \\(1/2\\) for orthogonal records, rather than 1 for identical records. Chapter 5 develops this joint-state calculation."}, {"title": "Basis coordinates and ket notation", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-1-1", "text": "4.1.1 Basis coordinates and ket notation § In a chosen orthonormal basis, a qubit has coordinates \\(\\psi=(\\alpha,\\beta)^T\\). Dirac notation writes the vector as \\(\\ket\\psi=\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\), where \\(\\ket0=(1,0)^T\\) and \\(\\ket1=(0,1)^T\\). The ket brackets name a vector, not a new operation. A basis label names a possible outcome of the corresponding basis measurement. A superposition is a linear combination relative to that basis; the same vector can itself be a basis vector in another basis. “Being in a superposition” therefore requires specifying the reference basis. The two coordinates are ordered by the selected basis. The coefficients belong to basis vectors, not to two simultaneous measurement outcomes. Problems What are the coordinate vectors of \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) in the computational basis? Write \\(3\\ket0+4\\ket1\\) as a column vector. Which coordinate belongs to each basis vector? If two people write different coordinate vectors for the same state in different bases, are they making different physical claims? What must you know before comparing their lists of amplitudes?"}, {"title": "Complex inner products and bra notation", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-1-2", "text": "4.1.2 Complex inner products and bra notation § For complex vectors, define \\(\\braket\\phi\\psi=\\sum_j\\phi_j^*\\psi_j\\). The bra \\(\\bra\\phi\\) is the conjugate-transpose row that performs this operation. Conjugation ensures \\(\\braket\\psi\\psi=\\sum_j|\\psi_j|^2\\geq0\\). For a unit vector \\(\\phi\\), the overlap is the coefficient of the component along \\(\\phi\\); the projected vector is \\(\\ket\\phi\\braket\\phi\\psi\\). The coefficient can be complex, so a picture of a real-plane shadow illustrates only a restricted case. Orthogonality means zero overlap and will correspond to perfectly distinguishable basis alternatives. For \\(v=(1,i)\\), the conjugate transpose is \\((1,-i)\\). Multiplying gives \\(1+1=2\\). Omitting conjugation would incorrectly give zero. Problems What extra operation distinguishes a complex inner product from an ordinary coordinate-wise product? Calculate the squared norm of \\((1,i)^T\\) by conjugating the first vector before multiplying. Why is complex conjugation necessary in the inner product? Test what would happen to the proposed squared length of the one-component vector \\(i\\) if conjugation were omitted."}, {"title": "Changes of basis", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-1-3", "text": "4.1.3 Changes of basis § Let \\(\\ket\\pm=(\\ket0\\pm\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). These form an orthonormal basis. The state \\(\\ket0\\) is also \\((\\ket++\\ket-)/\\sqrt2\\). Its coordinates changed, not its physical preparation. If the columns of \\(B\\) are the new basis vectors expressed in old coordinates, the new coordinates are \\(B^\\dagger\\psi\\). This passive change of description must be distinguished from physically applying a gate. Applying \\(B^\\dagger\\) and then measuring in the old basis does, however, implement a measurement in the new basis. That connection will become useful in circuits. These rows change coordinates without changing the physical state. The plus/minus basis is defined by \\(\\ket\\pm=(\\ket0\\pm\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Problems What are \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) in the computational basis? Add the expressions for \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) to write \\(\\ket0\\) in that basis. Writing a state in a new basis and applying a gate can use similar matrix calculations. How would you distinguish a change in description from a physical change when predicting a fixed measurement?"}, {"title": "Pure states and normalization", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-2-1", "text": "4.2.1 Pure states and normalization § The pure-state postulate represents a system by a unit vector in a complex inner-product space, with vectors differing only by global phase representing the same physical state. Thus \\(\\sum_j|\\alpha_j|^2=1\\). A qubit can be written \\(\\cos(\\theta/2)\\ket0+e^{i\\phi}\\sin(\\theta/2)\\ket1\\). The squared magnitudes specify one measurement’s probabilities, while relative phase affects other measurements. Not every preparation has a pure-state description: classical uncertainty about preparation or entanglement with another system can require a density matrix. The main text first treats pure preparations and complete joint systems. The vector \\((3,4)\\) has norm five. Dividing each coordinate by five gives probabilities \\(9/25\\) and \\(16/25\\), which add to one. Problems What is the required norm of a pure-state vector? Normalize \\(3\\ket0+4\\ket1\\) and check that the resulting measurement probabilities sum to one. A vector and twice that vector point in the same direction. Why cannot both be used directly as normalized state vectors? Which part of the vector describes the state, and what role does normalization play in assigning probabilities?"}, {"title": "Projective measurement and conditional states", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-2-2", "text": "4.2.2 Projective measurement and conditional states § For a projective measurement with orthogonal projectors \\(P_j\\) summing to identity, the Born rule gives \\[p(j)=\\|P_j\\ket\\psi\\|^2.\\] Conditional on an outcome with nonzero probability, the state becomes \\(P_j\\ket\\psi/\\sqrt{p(j)}\\). For one-dimensional projectors \\(P_j=\\ket{e_j}\\bra{e_j}\\), this is measurement along orthonormal directions. If \\(\\ket\\psi=(\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0+(1/2)\\ket1\\), the outcomes have probabilities \\(3/4\\) and \\(1/4\\). One run produces one outcome, not both fractional answers. Restoring unit length describes the state after conditioning on that outcome. A real-coordinate illustration of the Born rule. The projections are components used to calculate probabilities, not simultaneous fractional measurement results. Problems What does a computational-basis measurement return? For \\((\\sqrt3/2)\\ket0+(1/2)\\ket1\\), calculate each outcome probability and state the conditional state after outcome 1. A measurement can have a multidimensional outcome subspace. Does observing its outcome determine a unique final vector? What information about the initial state can survive inside that subspace?"}, {"title": "Repeated measurements on independently prepared states", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-2-3", "text": "4.2.3 Repeated measurements on independently prepared states § Repeated measurements on independently prepared copies estimate a distribution. If an event has probability \\(p\\), its count in \\(N\\) independent trials has mean (expected count) \\(Np\\) and variance (mean squared deviation from that count) \\(Np(1-p)\\). The observed fraction has typical fluctuation of order \\(1/\\sqrt N\\), not exact equality to \\(p\\). This differs from measuring the same system twice without re-preparing it: after an ideal projective measurement, the same outcome is certain on immediate repetition in the same measurement. An experiment must specify both preparation and measurement before its counts can be interpreted. After observing zero in a computational-basis measurement, the same qubit remains in \\(\\ket0\\). A fresh \\(\\ket+\\) still gives an equal distribution. Problems What does “independently prepared copies” mean? Measure \\(\\ket+\\) in the computational basis and obtain 0. Compare the next measurement of that same qubit with a measurement of a fresh \\(\\ket+\\). Why is measuring one system repeatedly not generally equivalent to measuring many independently prepared copies? Which experiment would reveal the original preparation probabilities, and which would mostly test the state left by the first measurement?"}, {"title": "Operator action on a basis", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-3-1", "text": "4.3.1 Operator action on a basis § A linear transformation is determined by its action on basis vectors: \\(A\\sum_j\\alpha_j\\ket j=\\sum_j\\alpha_j A\\ket j\\). Those basis images are its matrix columns. For example, a real-plane shear with columns \\((1,0)^T\\) and \\((1,1)^T\\) moves the second basis direction while retaining the first. It illustrates matrix construction but is not a valid deterministic isolated-system quantum evolution because it does not preserve all lengths and overlaps. Linear algebra describes many maps; the physical postulate selects which ones describe closed evolution. The \\(X\\) operation is fixed by its two basis actions. Multiply those outputs by the input coefficients and add to obtain the output on a superposition. Problems What does linearity say about \\(U(a\\ket0+b\\ket1)\\)? Given \\(U\\ket0=\\ket1\\) and \\(U\\ket1=\\ket0\\), find \\(U\\) applied to \\((\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). Why does knowing an operator’s action on every basis vector determine its action on all superpositions? What could go wrong if a proposed rule were allowed to depend on how the same vector was decomposed?"}, {"title": "Adjoints and inner products", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-3-2", "text": "4.3.2 Adjoints and inner products § After applying \\(A\\) to a state, its overlap with reference \\(\\phi\\) is \\(\\braket\\phi{A\\psi}\\). The adjoint is defined by \\[\\braket\\phi{A\\psi}=\\braket{A^\\dagger\\phi}\\psi.\\] We can transform the state and keep the reference fixed, or transform the reference by the adjoint and keep the state fixed. In coordinates \\(A^\\dagger=(A^*)^T\\). For a rotation, the adjoint rotates backward. For a real stretch \\(A=\\operatorname{diag}(2,1)\\), it is the same stretch, not its inverse. The overlap identity, not “undoing,” is the general meaning of the adjoint. A unitary has an inverse equal to its adjoint. A projection onto \\(\\ket0\\) has an adjoint but loses the \\(\\ket1\\) component, so it has no full-space inverse. Problems How is a matrix adjoint formed? Find the adjoint of the diagonal matrix with entries \\(1\\) and \\(i\\). Multiply the two matrices and inspect the result. The adjoint is defined even when an inverse does not exist. What question about overlaps does it answer that is different from undoing an operation? Use a projection as a test case."}, {"title": "Unitary evolution", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-3-3", "text": "4.3.3 Unitary evolution § The evolution postulate assigns a unitary \\(U\\) to a closed system’s evolution: \\(U^\\dagger U=I\\). Consequently, \\[\\braket{U\\phi}{U\\psi}=\\braket\\phi\\psi.\\] Norms, total probability, and distinguishability through overlaps are preserved; the inverse is \\(U^\\dagger\\). For finite-dimensional linear maps on a fixed state space, preservation of every vector’s norm implies this condition. Normalizing each output separately after an arbitrary map would generally be nonlinear and does not establish a physical evolution rule. Measurement is not such an invertible map on the measured system; its outcomes and any environment must be treated separately. An inner product between two inputs equals that between their unitary outputs. Orthogonal inputs stay orthogonal; nonorthogonal inputs do not become perfectly distinguishable. Problems What equation must \\(U\\) satisfy to be unitary? The matrix \\(X\\) swaps two coordinates. Apply it twice and use the result to identify its inverse. Unitary evolution is reversible, while a measurement outcome can leave several possible inputs indistinguishable. Why does this not justify describing measurement as just another unitary on the measured system alone?"}, {"title": "Eigenvectors and eigenspaces", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-4-1", "text": "4.4.1 Eigenvectors and eigenspaces § An eigenvector satisfies \\(A\\ket v=\\lambda\\ket v\\). Its direction is preserved; its coefficient changes by \\(\\lambda\\). For \\(Z=\\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)\\), the basis vectors have eigenvalues \\(+1,-1\\). For \\(X\\), which exchanges zero and one, \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) have those eigenvalues. A vector in a unitary eigendirection gains only a phase, since the eigenvalue has magnitude one. Eigenvectors therefore identify especially simple responses to an operation and help us choose useful input states. For \\(Z\\), the computational basis vectors are eigenvectors with eigenvalues \\(+1\\) and \\(-1\\). A general superposition need not be an eigenvector. Problems What equation defines an eigenvector and its eigenvalue? \\(Z\\) maps \\(\\ket0\\) to \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) to \\(-\\ket1\\). Identify the two eigenvalues. If two independent eigenvectors have the same eigenvalue, what happens to their superposition under the operator? Can the eigenvalue alone distinguish the two vectors or the superposition?"}, {"title": "Hermitian operators and spectral projections", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-4-2", "text": "4.4.2 Hermitian operators and spectral projections § A Hermitian operator \\(A=A^\\dagger\\) has real eigenvalues and an orthonormal eigenbasis. Grouping equal eigenvalues gives the spectral decomposition \\(A=\\sum_j a_jP_j\\). In the associated ideal measurement, the numerical result is \\(a_j\\) and the probability is \\(\\|P_j\\psi\\|^2\\). Degeneracy means an outcome selects a subspace, not a unique vector. This makes precise the phrase “measure an observable”: specify both the eigenspaces used for projection and the numerical labels reported. Changing an observable can change outcome probabilities without changing the input preparation. For \\(A=\\mathrm{diag}(2,5)\\), the projectors select the two computational-basis components. The measurement reports the associated eigenvalue. Problems What makes an operator Hermitian? For the diagonal matrix with entries 2 and 5, identify its eigenvectors, eigenvalues, and computational-basis measurement projectors. Two Hermitian operators can share the same eigenspaces but assign them different eigenvalues. In what sense do their projective measurements ask the same question, and in what sense do they report different values?"}, {"title": "Nonorthogonal state discrimination", "chapter": "Quantum States and Measurement", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#topic-4-4-3", "text": "4.4.3 Nonorthogonal state discrimination § Perfectly distinguishing two pure states requires an outcome that identifies each with certainty. Such a measurement separates them into orthogonal outcome subspaces, so their overlap must be zero. Equivalently, a unitary interaction that produces perfectly distinct records cannot turn a nonzero initial overlap into zero, because unitaries preserve overlaps. Hence \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\), whose overlap is \\(1/\\sqrt2\\), cannot always be identified correctly from one unknown copy. This limit is structural, not a defect of a particular detector. General measurements can trade errors for inconclusive outcomes, but cannot evade this deterministic limit. The real slice shown contains \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\) at \\(45^\\circ\\). Their overlap is \\(1/\\sqrt2\\), so one copy cannot always reveal which was prepared. Angles here represent state-vector geometry, not physical space. Problems Can two nonorthogonal states always be distinguished perfectly from one copy? Calculate \\(\\braket{0}{+}\\). Does the result allow perfect discrimination of these two states? Nonorthogonal states are different, yet no measurement always identifies which one was supplied from a single copy. Why is mathematical distinctness weaker than perfect experimental distinguishability? Which requirement could you relax to obtain a different task?"}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-1", "text": "Does \\(U^\\dagger\\) always equal \\(U^{-1}\\)? No. The adjoint is the conjugate transpose. It equals the inverse when \\(U\\) is unitary. A nontrivial projection has an adjoint but no inverse on the whole space."}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-2", "text": "Normalize \\(3\\ket0+4i\\ket1\\) and calculate the probabilities of a computational-basis measurement. The normalized state is \\((3/5)\\ket0+(4i/5)\\ket1\\). The probabilities are \\(9/25\\) and \\(16/25\\). Conditional on outcome 1, the resulting ray is represented by \\(\\ket1\\); its overall phase has no observable effect."}, {"title": "Quantum States and Measurement — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "04-states-measurement.html#exercise-3", "text": "Why can no unitary map both \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket+\\) to two orthogonal output states? Unitary transformations preserve inner products. Two orthogonal outputs have inner product zero, but these inputs have inner product \\(1/\\sqrt2\\). Such a map cannot be unitary. This is part of the obstruction to perfect discrimination of nonorthogonal states."}, {"title": "Tensor products and product bases", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-1-1", "text": "5.1.1 Tensor products and product bases § Two qubits have joint basis labels \\(00,01,10,11\\). Write \\(\\ket{ab}=\\ket a\\otimes\\ket b\\), using the tensor product . Coordinates are ordered here with the first qubit as the leftmost bit. For individual vectors \\((a,b)^T\\) and \\((c,d)^T\\), their tensor product is \\((ac,ad,bc,bd)^T\\). Every pair of local coordinates contributes one joint coordinate. Dimensions multiply rather than add. An operation on the first qubit alone is \\(U\\otimes I\\); it acts on each pair of coefficients with the second label held fixed. The joint basis has one label for every pair of local labels. This is the Cartesian arrangement of tensor-product basis vectors, not a mixture. Problems List the computational-basis labels for two qubits. Expand \\(\\ket0\\otimes(\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) in the joint basis. Why does combining two two-dimensional systems require four joint basis labels rather than merely four alternatives to which system exists? What does a tensor-product label say about simultaneous properties of both systems?"}, {"title": "Product states", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-1-2", "text": "5.1.2 Product states § Independent pure preparations give a product state \\(\\ket\\psi\\otimes\\ket\\phi\\). The amplitude of a joint basis outcome is the product of the two local amplitudes, so its probability is the product of their probabilities. For example, \\(\\ket+\\otimes\\ket0=(\\ket{00}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\). Although two joint amplitudes are nonzero, the second qubit is certainly zero and the state factors. Superposition alone is not evidence of entanglement . The relevant question is whether one local vector for each subsystem reproduces every joint amplitude. Both terms share the same second-qubit state. Factoring it out shows that this pure state is a product, despite having two nonzero amplitudes. Problems What does it mean for a pure joint state to be a product state? Factor \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\) into one state for each qubit. A product state may have several nonzero joint amplitudes. Why is counting those amplitudes an unreliable test for entanglement? What relationship among them would establish that the state factors?"}, {"title": "The composition postulate", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-1-3", "text": "5.1.3 The composition postulate § The composition postulate assigns the tensor-product space to a composite system. A register of \\(n\\) qubits therefore has \\(2^n\\) basis coordinates. This is a statement about the mathematical description, not a promise that a measurement yields \\(2^n\\) numbers. A basis measurement returns one \\(n\\)-bit label. Maintaining a general state vector on a classical simulator can be expensive, but that fact alone does not prove an algorithmic advantage. We must still identify a task and a process that makes the required information observable efficiently. The number of basis coefficients doubles with each added qubit. One computational-basis measurement still returns only one n-bit label. Problems How many computational-basis states do three qubits have? For three qubits, compare the number of state-vector coordinates with the number of bits in one basis-measurement outcome. An \\(n\\)-qubit state has exponentially many coordinates, but a basis measurement returns only \\(n\\) bits. Why does the size of the mathematical description not by itself establish a computational advantage?"}, {"title": "Bell-state preparation", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-2-1", "text": "5.2.1 Bell-state preparation § Prepare \\(\\ket{00}\\), apply \\(H\\) to the first qubit, then CNOT with the first as control. The state changes as \\[\\ket{00}\\to\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{10}}{\\sqrt2}\\to\\frac{\\ket{00}+\\ket{11}}{\\sqrt2}.\\] The final vector is a Bell state , denoted \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\). The circuit drawing records this sequence, with time running left to right. The vertical CNOT connector acts on the joint state; it does not mean that the first qubit was measured to decide a classical action on the second. A Hadamard followed by CNOT prepares a Bell state. Read the circuit from left to right. Predict first. Reset to \\(\\ket{00}\\). After \\(H\\) on the first qubit, which two outcomes are possible? Which outcomes remain after CNOT? Experiment: build a Bell state Follow the joint state from \\(\\ket{00}\\) through a Hadamard on the first qubit and then CNOT. The four bars show computational-basis probabilities. Apply H to the first qubit Reset State: \\(\\ket{00}\\) Both qubits start in 0. The final pure state is entangled: its joint amplitudes do not factor into two single-qubit states. Its probabilities alone would not distinguish it from a classical mixture. After trying it: compare your explanation After \\(H\\), \\(00\\) and \\(10\\) each have probability \\(1/2\\). CNOT changes \\(10\\) to \\(11\\), so the final outcomes are \\(00\\) and \\(11\\), each with probability \\(1/2\\). The bars alone do not establish entanglement. Problems Which two gates prepare the Bell state from \\(\\ket{00}\\) in this subsection? Write the state after \\(H\\) on the first qubit and then after CNOT with that qubit as control. In Bell-state preparation, CNOT correlates the two qubits without measuring its control. How would the resulting preparation differ if the first qubit were measured after \\(H\\) and its outcome then used as an ordinary control?"}, {"title": "Nonfactorization of amplitudes", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-2-2", "text": "5.2.2 Nonfactorization of amplitudes § If \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\) factored as \\((a\\ket0+b\\ket1)\\otimes(c\\ket0+d\\ket1)\\), its coefficients would require \\(ac=bd=1/\\sqrt2\\) but \\(ad=bc=0\\). The first two equations make all four factors nonzero, contradicting the latter equations. The state is entangled. This elementary test establishes more than correlation in one chosen measurement: it rules out every product of pure local states. A classical mixture of \\(00\\) and \\(11\\) has the same zero–one counts, but different predictions in other bases. A product \\((a\\ket0+b\\ket1)(c\\ket0+d\\ket1)\\) would require \\(ac\\) and \\(bd\\) nonzero. Then all four factors are nonzero, contradicting \\(ad=bc=0\\). Problems What must fail for a pure two-qubit state to be entangled? Try factoring \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). Explain why nonzero \\(00\\) and \\(11\\) coefficients force unwanted cross terms in a product. Why is failure to factor amplitudes stronger evidence of pure-state entanglement than observing correlated outcomes in one basis? What comparison would expose the difference between a Bell state and a classical mixture?"}, {"title": "Joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-2-3", "text": "5.2.3 Joint, marginal, and conditional probabilities § For joint amplitudes \\(\\alpha_{ab}\\), \\(p(a,b)=|\\alpha_{ab}|^2\\). The marginal probability is \\(p(a)=\\sum_b p(a,b)\\); when \\(p(a)>0\\), \\(p(b\\mid a)=p(a,b)/p(a)\\). The Bell state gives \\(p(00)=p(11)=1/2\\) and equal local probabilities. It also equals \\((\\ket{++}+\\ket{--})/\\sqrt2\\), so equal \\(X\\)-basis outcomes are certain. The classical mixture of \\(00\\) and \\(11\\) instead gives independent fair \\(X\\) outcomes. Correlation alone in one basis does not establish coherent entanglement; the measurement choice matters. For the Bell state, the row sums give the first-qubit probabilities. Conditional on the first bit being zero, only the \\(00\\) entry remains possible. Problems How do you obtain a marginal probability from joint probabilities? For \\(p(00)=p(11)=1/2\\) and all other outcomes zero, calculate \\(p(\\text{first}=0)\\) and \\(p(\\text{second}=0 \\text{ given first}=0)\\). Knowing the first Bell measurement outcome lets you predict the second, although either local outcome is random. What changes when you condition on the first outcome: the local statistics available without the message, or the predictions available with it?"}, {"title": "Basis-state copying", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-3-1", "text": "5.3.1 Basis-state copying § CNOT maps \\(\\ket0\\ket0\\) to \\(\\ket0\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\ket0\\) to \\(\\ket1\\ket1\\). Thus it copies a computational basis label into a blank target. On a superposition, linearity gives \\(\\alpha\\ket{00}+\\beta\\ket{11}\\), not \\((\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1)^{\\otimes2}\\). The latter generally contains cross terms \\(\\ket{01}\\) and \\(\\ket{10}\\). Keeping the label-copying calculation explicit prevents a misleading inference from two successful basis tests to copying arbitrary quantum information. The first two rows copy computational-basis labels into a blank target. By linearity the third row produces an entangled state rather than two copies of \\(\\ket+\\). Problems Which target value allows CNOT to copy a basis label? Apply CNOT to \\(\\ket0\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\ket0\\), then use linearity to find its action on \\(\\ket+\\ket0\\). Why does a device that copies both basis states fail to copy their arbitrary superposition? Expand the desired two-copy output and identify the terms that linear evolution does not produce."}, {"title": "The no-cloning theorem", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-3-2", "text": "5.3.2 The no-cloning theorem § Suppose one unitary copied every unknown pure state into a blank register. For two possible inputs, overlap preservation would require \\[\\braket\\phi\\psi=(\\braket\\phi\\psi)^2.\\] For distinct nonorthogonal states the overlap is neither zero nor one, so the equation fails. The contradiction excludes a universal cloner. A device designed for a known orthogonal set can copy its labels; preparing a known state repeatedly also poses no problem. The restriction is on perfectly copying an arbitrary unknown state by one fixed operation. Let \\(c=\\braket\\psi\\phi\\). A unitary cloning map would preserve input overlap \\(c\\), while two copies have overlap \\(c^2\\). The value \\(1/\\sqrt2\\) cannot satisfy that requirement. Problems Does the no-cloning theorem forbid copying known orthogonal basis labels? Compare CNOT’s output on \\(\\ket+\\ket0\\) with \\(\\ket+\\ket+\\) by expanding both in the joint basis. What assumption in the no-cloning argument must change if the possible input states are known to be orthogonal? Why is a machine for copying that restricted set not a counterexample to the theorem?"}, {"title": "Local states and no-signalling", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-3-3", "text": "5.3.3 Local states and no-signalling § A subsystem of an entangled pair need not have a pure-state vector. Its local predictions can be summarized by a density matrix; for either half of \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\), it is \\(I/2\\). A local unitary or a measurement with its outcome ignored cannot change the other party’s marginal distribution. Conditioning on a communicated outcome can change the recipient’s description, but the information must arrive through a classical channel. This is why entanglement supplies correlations without an instantaneous signalling method. Appendix D gives the density-matrix calculation. For the Bell pair, either local computational-basis distribution is fair. Learning the remote outcome permits conditioning; without it, the local distribution remains unchanged. Problems What is the local outcome distribution for either half of a Bell state? If the other qubit is measured but its outcome is not sent to you, do your computational-basis probabilities change? Explain using the two possible outcomes. A distant measurement can change the state you assign after learning its outcome. Why can this not be used to send a chosen message without communicating the outcome? Distinguish conditional knowledge from locally observable statistics."}, {"title": "Entanglement resources", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-4-1", "text": "5.4.1 Entanglement resources § Alice holds an unknown qubit \\(\\ket\\psi=\\alpha\\ket0+\\beta\\ket1\\) and one half of a Bell pair; Bob holds the other half. The initial joint state is \\(\\ket\\psi\\otimes\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\). Alice applies CNOT from the input to her Bell half, then \\(H\\) to the input. No quantum system carrying the input is then sent to Bob. The shared entanglement and two later classical bits are the resources. To see why the protocol works, we must track the complete state rather than assume the Bell pair already contains the answer. Alice and Bob first share a Bell pair. Alice’s joint measurement and two-bit message allow Bob to select the correction; the shared pair is consumed in transferring the input state. Problems What shared quantum resource does teleportation require? List the shared quantum resource and the classical communication used to teleport one qubit. Why must shared entanglement be included among the resources used by teleportation? If preparing and distributing the entangled pair were costly, how would that change an assessment of the protocol?"}, {"title": "Measurement and conditional corrections", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-4-2", "text": "5.4.2 Measurement and conditional corrections § After Alice’s gates, the state is \\[\\frac12\\sum_{m,n\\in\\{0,1\\}}\\ket{mn}\\otimes X^nZ^m\\ket\\psi.\\] Alice’s measurement gives each pair \\((m,n)\\) with probability one quarter. Bob’s conditional state is \\(X^nZ^m\\ket\\psi\\); applying \\(Z^mX^n\\) recovers \\(\\ket\\psi\\). In temporal order he applies \\(X^n\\) first, then \\(Z^m\\). The circuit uses dashed classical controls after measurement. The formula accounts for every outcome, so teleportation succeeds without selecting a favorable measurement result. Alice’s two measured bits control Bob’s \\(X\\) and \\(Z\\) corrections. Dashed lines carry classical information; the Bell pair is prepared at the left. Problems Why does the receiver apply a correction in teleportation? If the receiver’s conditional state is \\(X\\ket\\psi\\), which gate recovers \\(\\ket\\psi\\)? Check by applying it. Before the classical correction, the receiver’s state depends on the sender’s measurement outcome. Why is knowing which correction to apply essential even though each possible correction is reversible?"}, {"title": "State transfer and classical communication", "chapter": "Composite Systems and Entanglement", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#topic-5-4-3", "text": "5.4.3 State transfer and classical communication § Before the two classical bits arrive, averaging Bob’s four possible conditional states gives \\(I/2\\), independent of \\(\\psi\\). He cannot extract a message about the input from that state alone. After correction, his qubit has the input state; Alice no longer retains that unknown state, and the original Bell pair has been consumed. Thus teleportation transfers quantum information without cloning it. It also illustrates a recurring discipline: distinguish a joint pure state, a state conditioned on a known result, and a local state when that result is unavailable. The two-bit message selects a correction using a previously shared Bell pair. It is not a classical list of the unknown amplitudes. Problems How many classical bits are sent in single-qubit teleportation? Explain why receiving the classical bits alone, without the shared Bell pair, is not the teleportation protocol. Teleportation communicates only two classical bits while transferring an arbitrary qubit state. Why does that not mean the two bits contain a classical description of all its amplitudes? What other resources make the transfer possible?"}, {"title": "Composite Systems and Entanglement — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#exercise-1", "text": "Is \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{01})/\\sqrt2\\) entangled? No. It is \\(\\ket0\\otimes(\\ket0+\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\), a product state. A sum of basis states is not automatically entangled."}, {"title": "Composite Systems and Entanglement — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#exercise-2", "text": "Apply \\(H\\) to the first qubit of \\(\\ket{00}\\), then CNOT with that qubit as control. Give joint and first-qubit probabilities in the computational basis. The intermediate state is \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt2\\) and the final state is \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\). The joint probabilities are \\(p(00)=p(11)=1/2\\) and \\(p(01)=p(10)=0\\). Summing over the second bit gives \\(p(\\text{first}=0)=p(\\text{first}=1)=1/2\\)."}, {"title": "Composite Systems and Entanglement — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "05-composite-systems.html#exercise-3", "text": "Compare measuring both qubits in the \\(X\\) basis for the Bell state \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\) and for an equal classical mixture of \\(00\\) and \\(11\\). For \\(\\ket{\\Phi^+}\\), the outcomes \\({+}{+}\\) and \\({-}{-}\\) each have probability \\(1/2\\). The mixture yields \\({+}{+}\\), \\({+}{-}\\), \\({-}{+}\\), and \\({-}{-}\\) with probability \\(1/4\\) each. Their computational-basis counts agree, but these \\(X\\)-basis counts distinguish coherence from the mixture."}, {"title": "Constant and balanced Boolean functions", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-1-1", "text": "6.1.1 Constant and balanced Boolean functions § Let \\(f:\\{0,1\\}\\to\\{0,1\\}\\). The task is to determine whether \\(f(0)=f(1)\\). Such a function is constant if they agree and balanced if they differ. For this one-bit domain these exhaust all possibilities; for larger domains, restricting attention to constant or balanced functions is a genuine promise. We need one property, not a printed list of both function values. This distinction between the requested answer and the full data guides the quantum algorithm . Deutsch’s task asks whether the two outputs agree. It does not require identifying which truth table in a class was supplied. Problems When is a one-bit Boolean function constant? Classify the tables \\((f(0),f(1))=(0,0)\\), \\((0,1)\\), \\((1,0)\\), and \\((1,1)\\) as constant or balanced. For a one-bit function, the task asks whether two values agree, not what each value is. How can changing the requested answer change the information a successful algorithm must obtain?"}, {"title": "Classical query access", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-1-2", "text": "6.1.2 Classical query access § A classical query chooses \\(x\\) and receives \\(f(x)\\). One query cannot settle this task with certainty: either observed value is compatible with both a constant and a balanced function, depending on the unqueried value. Two queries suffice. The comparison is exact and uses a specified access interface. It does not claim that every classical computation related to the function needs two physical operations. If the function’s description is supplied in a different form, the access model and the comparison may change. If a classical query returns \\(f(0)=0\\), both a constant and a balanced truth table remain possible. A second value is needed for an exact classical answer. Problems How many values does one ordinary classical query reveal? You queried \\(f(0)\\) and obtained 0. Write one constant and one balanced function consistent with that observation. After one classical query, which two possible truth tables remain indistinguishable while belonging to different classes? Why does this pair obstruct an exact one-query decision procedure?"}, {"title": "Reversible quantum oracles", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-1-3", "text": "6.1.3 Reversible quantum oracles § The quantum interface is the reversible oracle \\[U_f\\ket{x,y}=\\ket{x,y\\oplus f(x)}.\\] On basis states it permutes labels and is its own inverse, so its linear extension is unitary . The input register is retained. A query means one use of this specified operation, even when the input is a superposition . The black-box model counts uses without charging for the internal construction of \\(U_f\\). This abstraction isolates information access; later circuit analysis must restore costs hidden inside the box. For \\(f(x)=1\\), the two target values are swapped while \\(x\\) is kept. The full map is reversible even when the function \\(f\\) itself is not one-to-one. Problems What is the reversible oracle’s action on \\(\\ket{x}\\ket{t}\\)? For \\(f(0)=1\\), find the oracle outputs on \\(\\ket0\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket0\\ket1\\). Why must the oracle specify what happens to its target register, not merely declare that it “returns \\(f(x)\\)”? How does retaining \\(x\\) permit a reversible operation even when \\(f\\) is many-to-one?"}, {"title": "Oracle action on superpositions", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-2-1", "text": "6.2.1 Oracle action on superpositions § With a clean target, a query gives \\[U_f\\left(\\frac{\\ket0+\\ket1}{\\sqrt2}\\otimes\\ket0\\right)=\\frac{\\ket{0,f(0)}+\\ket{1,f(1)}}{\\sqrt2}.\\] This follows directly from linearity and the basis definition. There is no step that individually reads both outputs into a classical memory. The state contains coherent relationships among amplitudes. Whether those relationships help depends on the next transformation and on which measurement is made. With \\(f(0)=0\\) and \\(f(1)=1\\), a blank target becomes correlated with the input. Linearity determines the output on the entire superposition. Problems Which rule lets us find an oracle’s action on a superposition from its action on basis states? For \\(f(0)=0\\) and \\(f(1)=1\\), apply the oracle to \\(\\ket+\\ket0\\) by expanding the input into two terms. An oracle acts on every basis component of a superposition. Why is that not equivalent to receiving a readable list of all function values? Which subsequent step determines whether this action is useful?"}, {"title": "Register measurement", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-2-2", "text": "6.2.2 Register measurement § Measuring both registers immediately returns one random pair \\((x,f(x))\\). It does not return the two values required for the classical exact solution. Preparing the same state again can yield more samples, but sampling is not the desired one-query advantage. A useful algorithm must convert the property “equal or different” into a distinction between measurable final states. Simply evaluating in superposition has not finished the computation. For \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\), a computational-basis measurement samples one of these four labels. It does not output the full list of amplitudes. Problems Does one basis measurement return every amplitude of a register? For \\((\\ket{00}+\\ket{11})/\\sqrt2\\), list the possible measured pairs and their probabilities. If measuring the input register returns one sampled input, what becomes of the other function values encoded in the joint state? Why does the existence of those components not guarantee access to all of them in one run?"}, {"title": "Workspace entanglement", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-2-3", "text": "6.2.3 Workspace entanglement § If \\(f(0)\\ne f(1)\\), the target stores orthogonal records of the two inputs. Applying \\(H\\) only to the input cannot make those distinct joint alternatives cancel each other as though their target records were identical. Their contributions must still be grouped by the complete output label. This is the same path-record effect introduced in Chapter 3. Rather than discard the record, we can choose a target preparation that returns to itself while transferring the function information into phase. Different workspace records can prevent the desired input interference. The shared-workspace example factors; the branch-record example does not. The common normalization factor \\(1/\\sqrt2\\) is omitted in the labels. Problems What is a workspace record? Compare \\((\\ket0\\ket0+\\ket1\\ket0)/\\sqrt2\\) with \\((\\ket0\\ket0+\\ket1\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\). In which state does the second register distinguish the first-register alternatives? Two input branches leave different workspace records. Why can that obstruct the interference you wanted between them? What would have to be true of the final workspace for it to stop distinguishing the branches?"}, {"title": "Bit-flip eigenstates", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-3-1", "text": "6.3.1 Bit-flip eigenstates § The state \\(\\ket-=(\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) satisfies \\(X\\ket-=-\\ket-\\). Therefore a conditional bit flip multiplies this target by a sign without changing its physical state. If no flip occurs, the sign is positive. This eigenvector property is precisely what the oracle’s target needs. It is not enough to call the target a “minus state”; the calculation \\(X(\\ket0-\\ket1)=\\ket1-\\ket0\\) supplies the reason it transfers a bit value into a sign. A bit flip leaves \\(\\ket+\\) unchanged but multiplies \\(\\ket-\\) by \\(-1\\). Using the latter as the target makes a controlled flip supply a phase to the input branch. Problems What are the \\(X\\) eigenvalues of \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket-\\)? Apply \\(X\\) to \\((\\ket0-\\ket1)/\\sqrt2\\) and factor out the sign. Both \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) are eigenstates of the bit flip. Why does only one of them provide the sign change needed here? What property of the eigenvalue, rather than simply being an eigenstate, matters?"}, {"title": "Phase-oracle construction", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-3-2", "text": "6.3.2 Phase-oracle construction § For each input label, \\[U_f\\ket x\\ket-=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x\\ket-.\\] With input \\(\\ket+\\), the result is \\([(-1)^{f(0)}\\ket0+(-1)^{f(1)}\\ket1]\\ket-/\\sqrt2\\). The target factors out in the same state for every input, so it carries no distinguishable record of which branch occurred. The signs now belong to the input amplitudes. Equal signs differ from two positive signs only by a global phase ; opposite signs are a relative phase difference. This is phase kickback . The target returns to the same minus state, while the input receives a function-dependent phase. Problems What phase factor is attached to input \\(x\\) by a phase oracle? For \\(f(0)=0\\) and \\(f(1)=1\\), apply the phase factors to \\(\\ket+\\) and identify the new input state. Complementing every value of a Boolean function changes every phase mark by a common minus sign. Which distinctions between functions are therefore unavailable from this phase action alone?"}, {"title": "Coherent uncomputation", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-3-3", "text": "6.3.3 Coherent uncomputation § For a general predicate computed using workspace, apply a phase to its computed answer and then reverse the computation. If \\(A\\ket{x,0}=\\ket{x,f(x),g(x)}\\), the combined operation \\(A^\\dagger Z_{\\mathrm{answer}}A\\) maps \\(\\ket{x,0}\\) to \\((-1)^{f(x)}\\ket{x,0}\\). Workspace is restored while the sign remains. This is a phase-oracle construction from a value computation, not a free replacement: it uses the computation and its inverse. Counting only the final phase box would conceal those implementation costs. For a basis input \\(x\\), compute \\(f(x)\\) into clean workspace, apply \\(Z\\) to that bit, and reverse the computation. The function-dependent sign remains while the workspace returns to zero. Problems What operation clears workspace by undoing its computation? A reversible computation maps \\(\\ket{x}\\ket{0}\\) to \\(\\ket{x}\\ket{g(x)}\\). Write the action of its inverse on that output. Why is erasing workspace by measurement generally different from uncomputing it coherently? What record or disturbance could remain after the first method that the second is designed to avoid?"}, {"title": "Interference and readout", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-4-1", "text": "6.4.1 Interference and readout § Apply \\(H\\) to the input after kickback. Writing \\(s_x=(-1)^{f(x)}\\), the resulting input state is \\[\\frac{s_0+s_1}{2}\\ket0+\\frac{s_0-s_1}{2}\\ket1.\\] Equal signs give outcome zero with certainty; opposite signs give outcome one with certainty. The final Hadamard combines the two alternatives so that the unused outcome cancels. This equation shows both the output and the mechanism: the relative phase, rather than either function value separately, determines the answer. Ignoring a common global sign, constant functions leave the input in \\(\\ket+\\) and balanced functions produce \\(\\ket-\\). The final \\(H\\) converts this distinction into a definite basis outcome. Problems What role does the last Hadamard play in Deutsch’s algorithm? Calculate \\(H\\ket+\\) and \\(H\\ket-\\). Match the two results to constant and balanced functions. The oracle encodes the distinction in signs, but the final measurement reads a bit. What exactly does the last Hadamard do to bridge those descriptions? Why would measuring before it fail?"}, {"title": "Quantum and classical query counts", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-4-2", "text": "6.4.2 Quantum and classical query counts § Deutsch’s algorithm prepares \\(\\ket0\\ket1\\), applies \\(H\\) to each qubit, queries \\(U_f\\), applies \\(H\\) to the input, and measures that input. It distinguishes constant from balanced using one quantum query, whereas an exact classical method needs two. Both statements concern the same oracle task and demand certainty in the ideal model. The circuit’s other gates and preparation are not queries, but they are real operations. A query separation is a clean first example of quantum advantage , not a complete hardware performance claim. Deutsch’s algorithm: prepare, query, recombine, and measure. Only the joint U_f box counts as an oracle query. Predict first. Compare a constant function with a balanced function. Before running the circuit, predict whether the final input bit is 0 or 1. Would complementing every function value change that bit? Experiment: an oracle changes relative phase Prepare the input in \\(\\ket{+}\\) and the target in \\(\\ket{-}\\). The oracle leaves the target unchanged and multiplies each input amplitude by \\((-1)^{f(x)}\\). A final Hadamard makes the relative sign observable. Function f(0) = 0, f(1) = 0 f(0) = 1, f(1) = 1 f(0) = 0, f(1) = 1 f(0) = 1, f(1) = 0 Apply oracle Reset |0⟩ +1/√2 |1⟩ +1/√2 Both input amplitudes have the same sign. Choose a function, then apply its oracle. A common sign changes no probabilities. Opposite signs change the result after the final Hadamard. After trying it: compare your explanation A constant function gives 0; a balanced function gives 1. Complementing the function multiplies the state after the phase oracle by a common minus sign, so it does not change the final measurement. Problems How many oracle calls does the ideal Deutsch algorithm use? Compare one quantum call with the two calls required by an exact classical method. Name one cost this count does not include. Deutsch’s algorithm uses one quantum query where exact classical computation needs two. Which access and success assumptions make that comparison meaningful? What extra information would be needed to compare total running times?"}, {"title": "Preparation, oracle evaluation, and measurement", "chapter": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#topic-6-4-3", "text": "6.4.3 Preparation, oracle evaluation, and measurement § The reusable strategy is to prepare coherent alternatives, encode a relevant property in relative amplitudes, recombine them, and measure in a basis that exposes the distinction. Correctness requires tracing the state through all four stages, including workspace. A circuit can be short and still fail if the final states for different answers are not distinguishable. The next chapter uses a related idea for a different task: finding a marked input. A phase mark will again be the beginning, not the completed answer. The target \\(\\ket-\\) lets the oracle encode a phase without keeping an input-dependent target record. The final \\(H\\) converts that phase into the answer. Problems Which target state is prepared for phase kickback? Replace \\(\\ket-\\) with \\(\\ket+\\) and use \\(X\\ket+=\\ket+\\). Does the target flip now give the input a function-dependent sign? If the oracle is correct but the target is prepared in \\(\\ket+\\) instead of \\(\\ket-\\), where does the algorithm lose its ability to distinguish the functions? What does this reveal about treating preparation as part of correctness?"}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-1", "text": "Why use \\(\\ket-\\) rather than \\(\\ket0\\) as the target for phase kickback? A flip changes \\(\\ket-\\) only by a sign. The same target state remains, while the input amplitudes acquire function-dependent signs. A \\(\\ket0\\) target instead records the function value and can become entangled with the input."}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-2", "text": "Take \\(f(0)=1\\) and \\(f(1)=0\\). Starting with \\(\\ket+\\ket-\\), apply the oracle and then \\(H\\) to the input. What is measured on the input? The oracle gives \\(-\\ket-\\ket-\\). The final \\(H\\) on the input gives \\(-\\ket1\\ket-\\). The overall minus sign changes no probabilities, so outcome 1 occurs with certainty: the function is balanced."}, {"title": "Quantum Queries and Phase Kickback — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "06-queries-phase-kickback.html#exercise-3", "text": "Replace \\(f\\) by its complement \\(1-f\\). Can Deutsch’s final measurement tell them apart? No. All input amplitudes gain the same extra minus sign, a global phase. The constant/balanced classification is unchanged. Deutsch’s measurement extracts that classification, not the individual truth table."}, {"title": "Search predicates and classical queries", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-1-1", "text": "7.1.1 Search predicates and classical queries § Search a set of \\(N\\) labels using a predicate \\(f(x)\\) that is one on marked items. An ordinary query tests a candidate. With one uniformly located marked item, classical search needs order \\(N\\) queries for constant success probability; checking all items settles the worst case. Begin with \\(N=4\\) and mark \\(11\\). We count access to the predicate, while keeping state preparation and other operations visible. The task is to output a marked label, not merely estimate the number of them. In this four-item example only \\(11\\) is marked. Testing a supplied candidate is different from locating the successful one. Problems What does a search predicate tell you about a candidate? Among eight equally likely candidates, two are marked. What is the success probability of one uniformly random guess? Why does a fast test of a candidate not automatically give a fast way to find a successful candidate? What additional structure would allow classical search to do better than inspecting unrelated possibilities?"}, {"title": "State preparation and phase marking", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-1-2", "text": "7.1.2 State preparation and phase marking § Two Hadamards prepare \\(\\ket s=(\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10}+\\ket{11})/2\\). A phase oracle \\(O_f\\ket x=(-1)^{f(x)}\\ket x\\) changes the amplitudes to \\((1,1,1,-1)/2\\). Every squared magnitude is still one quarter. Measuring now gains nothing. The oracle has created a relative sign that a subsequent operation can use. A phase oracle may be supplied directly or built by kickback or compute–phase–uncompute; its query cost must be stated accordingly. The marked item is \\(11\\). Every immediate measurement probability stays \\(1/4\\), so another coherent operation is needed to turn the sign into an advantage. Problems Does changing an amplitude’s sign change its squared magnitude? Start with four amplitudes of \\(1/2\\) and negate the marked one. Calculate its probability before and after marking. A phase mark leaves all immediate basis-measurement probabilities unchanged. Why can it still be a useful search operation? What must the next operation make those signs interact with?"}, {"title": "Reflection and final amplitudes", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-1-3", "text": "7.1.3 Reflection and final amplitudes § Apply \\(D=2\\ket s\\bra s-I\\). For an amplitude list \\(a_x\\), it sends each entry to \\(2\\mu-a_x\\), where \\(\\mu\\) denotes the arithmetic mean of the entries. After marking, the mean is \\(1/4\\): the three \\(+1/2\\) entries become zero, and the \\(-1/2\\) entry becomes one. One iteration \\(DO_f\\) therefore finds the marked label with certainty. The operation is a reflection about the prepared direction; it is not a measurement that estimates the mean from samples. A complete two-qubit search for 11. The first controlled-Z marks 11. The remaining coherent gates implement diffusion up to global phase. Both measured bits are one. Problems What is the reflection-about-the-mean rule for an amplitude \\(a\\)? For amplitudes \\((-1/2,1/2,1/2,1/2)\\), find the mean and apply \\(2\\cdot\\text{mean}-a\\) to each entry. For four items, one reflection after marking can make the marked amplitude one. Why does the same single iteration not generally give certainty for a larger search space? Track how the mean changes."}, {"title": "Good and bad subspaces", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-2-1", "text": "7.2.1 Good and bad subspaces § Suppose \\(0 For one marked item among four, \\(\\ket{G}=\\ket{11}\\) and \\(\\ket{B}=(\\ket{00}+\\ket{01}+\\ket{10})/\\sqrt3\\). These orthogonal directions contain the initial state and the entire ideal search trajectory. Problems What distinguishes the good subspace from the bad subspace? In a four-item search with only \\(00\\) marked, identify the good basis state and list the bad basis states. Why can a search over many basis states be described as motion in a two-dimensional plane? Which symmetries of the preparation and operations keep the state inside that plane?"}, {"title": "Initial success amplitude", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-2-2", "text": "7.2.2 Initial success amplitude § Write \\(\\sin\\theta=\\sqrt{M/N}\\), with \\(0<\\theta<\\pi/2\\). Then \\(\\ket s=\\sin\\theta\\ket G+\\cos\\theta\\ket B\\). Its initial success probability is \\(\\sin^2\\theta=M/N\\). The geometry uses the bad direction as angle zero and the good direction as angle \\(\\pi/2\\). This choice fixes signs in the rotation calculation. Although the underlying register may have many amplitudes, the two reflections preserve this plane, so one angle is enough to track the entire search evolution. With one marked item among four, \\(\\sin\\theta=1/2\\), so \\(\\theta=30^\\circ\\). The good amplitude is the vertical component; the success probability is its square. Problems If the initial success probability is \\(p\\), what is the nonnegative good amplitude? For one marked item among four equally weighted items, find \\(p\\) and \\(\\sqrt p\\). Use \\(\\sin\\theta=\\sqrt p\\) to find \\(\\theta\\). Two search problems have the same number of items but different marked fractions. Why do they begin at different angles to the bad direction? How does that alter the amount of rotation needed?"}, {"title": "Reflections and rotations", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-2-3", "text": "7.2.3 Reflections and rotations § The oracle negates the good component and retains the bad component: a reflection across the bad axis. The diffusion operation reflects across \\(\\ket s\\). Acting in this order, their product increases the state’s angle from the bad axis by \\(2\\theta\\). Reflection matrices also verify the result algebraically. A geometric drawing is useful because it explains both the increase in success probability and its eventual decrease: a rotation continues past the good axis unless we stop it. For \\(\\theta=30^\\circ\\), the initial state is at \\(30^\\circ\\). The oracle reflects it to \\(-30^\\circ\\) about the bad axis; reflection about the initial direction then sends it to \\(90^\\circ\\). These are real coordinates in the good/bad plane. Problems What geometric operation results from composing the two search reflections? For \\(\\theta=\\pi/6\\), start at angle \\(\\theta\\) and add \\(2\\theta\\) for one Grover iteration. What angle do you reach? Two reflections produce a rotation, but reversing their order reverses its direction. Why must an algorithm specify the order of the marking and preparation reflections rather than merely list them?"}, {"title": "Amplitude evolution", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-3-1", "text": "7.3.1 Amplitude evolution § After \\(k\\) Grover iterations, \\[\\ket{\\psi_k}=\\sin((2k+1)\\theta)\\ket G+\\cos((2k+1)\\theta)\\ket B,\\qquad p_k=\\sin^2((2k+1)\\theta).\\] The initial angle contributes one \\(\\theta\\); each iteration contributes two more. All marked labels have equal amplitudes in this uniform-search setting, so conditional on success the result is uniform over the marked set. The success probability depends on the marked fraction, not on which labels happen to be marked. For \\(N=4\\) and \\(M=1\\), \\(p_k=\\sin^2((2k+1)\\pi/6)\\). One iteration is ideal, but the next two return to probability \\(1/4\\). Stopping is part of the algorithm. Problems What formula gives success probability after \\(k\\) Grover iterations? With \\(\\theta=\\pi/6\\), evaluate \\(\\sin^2((2k+1)\\theta)\\) for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\). What does the oscillation of success probability tell you about the difference between coherent amplification and a process that irreversibly accumulates successful outcomes? Why need success not grow monotonically?"}, {"title": "Iteration count and overshooting", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-3-2", "text": "7.3.2 Iteration count and overshooting § Choose a nonnegative integer \\(k\\) near \\(\\pi/(4\\theta)-1/2\\) to bring the first peak close to the good axis. For a small marked fraction, \\(\\theta\\approx\\sqrt{M/N}\\), yielding order \\(\\sqrt{N/M}\\) iterations. Integer rounding usually prevents exact certainty; the four-item, one-mark case is special. More iterations are not always better. With \\(N=4,M=1\\), the probabilities for \\(k=0,1,2\\) are \\(1/4,1,1/4\\). This illustrates why the stopping rule is part of the algorithm rather than an implementation detail. Predict first. Set Search space to 4 and reset. There is one marked item; start from equal amplitudes. Predict the success probability after one iteration and after a second. Does another iteration always help? Experiment: rotate toward the marked item There is one marked item. A full Grover iteration rotates the state by \\(2\\theta\\), where \\(\\sin\\theta=1/\\sqrt N\\). Keep stepping after the first peak to see why overshooting matters. Search space 4 16 64 256 One iteration Play Reset Iterations 0 unmarked component → marked component ↑ Probability of finding the marked item: 6.25%. The initial state spreads probability uniformly over all items. After trying it: compare your explanation One iteration gives success probability 1. A second gives \\(1/4\\): continued rotation can move the state away from the marked direction. Compare this with the initial probability \\(1/4\\). Problems Can another Grover iteration reduce success probability? For \\(\\theta=\\pi/6\\), compare success at \\(k=1\\) with success at \\(k=2\\) using the iteration formula. An iteration rule can increase success initially and decrease it later. What information is needed to choose a stopping point? Why is “keep improving until certain” not an adequate instruction here?"}, {"title": "Unknown marked fraction and verification", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-3-3", "text": "7.3.3 Unknown marked fraction and verification § If the marked fraction is unknown, a fixed peak estimate may overshoot. A standard approach chooses a random iteration count from a gradually expanding range, measures, and verifies the candidate. For \\(M>0\\), suitable schedules retain expected order \\(\\sqrt{N/M}\\) query cost. If \\(M=0\\), verification never succeeds; an unbounded search cannot certify absence. A bounded-error decision version uses a bounded schedule and reports failure with a specified error guarantee when marked items exist. Distinguish that guarantee from an exact proof that none exists. If all items are marked, direct sampling already succeeds. Further detail: Boyer, Brassard, Høyer, and Tapp: Tight bounds on quantum searching . A failed verification rejects that candidate, not every possible candidate. With an unknown marked fraction, the schedule must choose how long to search before measuring again. Problems What does verification check after a candidate is measured? A measured candidate fails the predicate. Does that prove that every candidate fails? Explain with a four-item example. Why does verifying a candidate solve a different problem from knowing how many amplification steps to use? If a run fails verification, what has it established—and what has it not established—about the existence of a marked item?"}, {"title": "Arbitrary state preparation", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-4-1", "text": "7.4.1 Arbitrary state preparation § Uniform preparation is not essential. Let \\(A\\ket0=\\ket\\psi\\), and let \\(P_G\\) project onto the states counted as successful. If \\(0 A nonuniform preparation can still be resolved into normalized good and bad components. This example has \\(p=1/4\\), so its amplitudes are \\(1/2\\) and \\(\\sqrt3/2\\) regardless of how many underlying labels contribute. Problems Must amplitude amplification start from a uniform superposition? A preparation succeeds with probability \\(1/4\\). Identify its good and bad amplitude magnitudes using \\(\\sqrt p\\) and \\(\\sqrt{1-p}\\). If an initial preparation already favors good answers, how should that change amplification? Which feature of the preparation matters more than the raw number of basis states?"}, {"title": "Reflection using preparation and its inverse", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-4-2", "text": "7.4.2 Reflection using preparation and its inverse § Reflection about the prepared state is \\[R_\\psi=2\\ket\\psi\\bra\\psi-I=A(2\\ket0\\bra0-I)A^\\dagger.\\] Applied to an existing state, the rightmost inverse acts first. Pair this with \\(I-2P_G\\), which negates successful components. Their product rotates toward the good direction just as in search. The construction requires coherent access to \\(A\\), its inverse, and the success test. A destructive measurement of success is not a replacement for the coherent phase reflection. The inverse preparation acts first. Conjugating the zero-state reflection produces reflection about the prepared state. Problems Which inverse appears in the reflection \\(A(2\\ketbra00-I)A^\\dagger\\)? Apply this reflection to \\(A\\ket0\\) by first using \\(A^\\dagger A=I\\). Does the prepared state change sign? Why does implementing reflection about a prepared state require access to the preparation’s inverse? Is being able to obtain a copy of that state by an uncontrolled process enough to build the same reflection?"}, {"title": "Success probability and resource cost", "chapter": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#topic-7-4-3", "text": "7.4.3 Success probability and resource cost § For small \\(p\\), direct independent trials need order \\(1/p\\) preparations for constant success probability. Amplitude amplification needs order \\(1/\\sqrt p\\) iterations, each using the preparation, its inverse, and a coherent success test. If their costs are \\(C_A\\) and \\(C_G\\), the resulting cost is of order \\((C_A+C_G)/\\sqrt p\\), under the stated access assumptions. Verification and iteration selection must also be included. The quadratic improvement is in repeated use of these resources; expensive state loading or oracle construction can dominate the total runtime. For small \\(p\\), the improvement concerns repeated use of coherent preparation and the success test. Their implementation costs must be included. Problems What costs besides iteration count enter amplitude amplification? Suppose each round uses 12 gates and the initial preparation uses 8. Calculate the cost of five rounds, ignoring measurement. A better initial success probability can reduce the number of amplification rounds, yet its preparation may be expensive. How would you decide whether the improved preparation saves total work?"}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-1", "text": "Does applying a phase oracle immediately increase a marked outcome’s probability? No. The oracle changes a sign, not its squared magnitude. A subsequent reflection converts that phase difference into a change of amplitudes."}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-2", "text": "For four equal amplitudes of \\(1/2\\) with one marked item, calculate one marking step and one reflection about the mean. The marked amplitude becomes \\(1/2-(-1/2)=1\\). Each unmarked amplitude becomes \\(1/2-1/2=0\\). Measuring returns the marked item with certainty after one ideal iteration."}, {"title": "Quantum Search and Amplitude Amplification — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "07-amplitude-amplification.html#exercise-3", "text": "In that four-item example, what happens if you apply a second Grover iteration before measuring? The success probability falls to \\(\\sin^2(5\\pi/6)=1/4\\). More iterations need not improve the result: the state rotates past the good direction."}, {"title": "Wires and gate ordering", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-1-1", "text": "8.1.1 Wires and gate ordering § A circuit displays a sequence of operations with time increasing left to right. Each horizontal wire identifies a subsystem; a box denotes an operation. A wire does not carry a definite classical value when it belongs to a superposition or an entangled register. To interpret a circuit, track the joint vector between columns. If the first gate is \\(U\\) and the next is \\(V\\), the final state is \\(VU\\ket\\psi\\). Gates on disjoint wires act by tensor products and commute with one another, though a particular hardware schedule may impose additional constraints. The circuit applies \\(H\\) first and \\(Z\\) second. On a column vector the rightmost matrix acts first, so the combined operator is \\(ZH\\). Problems In which direction does time run in the circuit drawings? A circuit applies \\(H\\) and then \\(Z\\). Write the matrix product acting on the input column vector in the correct order. Why can interchanging two adjacent gates change the result even when the set of gates is unchanged? Give a condition under which the interchange is safe and explain why wire labels matter."}, {"title": "Circuits for query and search algorithms", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-1-2", "text": "8.1.2 Circuits for query and search algorithms § The earlier algorithms now supply concrete reading exercises. In Deutsch’s circuit, the first \\(H\\) pair prepares the query state; the oracle encodes signs; the last \\(H\\) exposes their relation. In search, the repeated block consists of a phase oracle followed by reflection about the prepared state. Circuit notation records how to compose operations, but a box labelled \\(O_f\\) is still an abstract oracle until its implementation is specified. Drawing a box does not prove its cost is one elementary gate. A query diagram counts uses of an oracle. Replacing each box by a circuit exposes the gate cost, plus any operations between calls. Problems What does an oracle box stand for in a query circuit? A circuit uses three oracle boxes, each requiring ten gates when expanded. Count the gates contributed by those boxes. A circuit diagram can represent an entire oracle by one box. What must be specified before you can infer an implementation cost from that diagram? Which conclusions can still be drawn while the box remains abstract?"}, {"title": "Measurement and classical control", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-1-3", "text": "8.1.3 Measurement and classical control § A measurement symbol terminates coherent evolution of that measured output within the diagram’s description and produces a classical result. Dashed lines here carry classical bits controlling later operations; solid lines carry quantum systems. A classical conditional correction means that the experimenter chooses a gate using a known outcome. It differs from a coherent controlled gate acting on an unmeasured superposition. Teleportation uses both: a coherent CNOT before measurement and classically controlled corrections afterward. Classical control uses a recorded outcome. It selects a branch after measurement rather than preserving a coherent superposition of both control alternatives. Problems What kind of information controls a classically conditioned correction? A measurement produces bit \\(b\\), and \\(X\\) is applied only if \\(b=1\\). Write the operation performed for each possible \\(b\\). How does controlling an operation with a measured bit differ from controlling it coherently with a qubit? What happens to the possibility of interference between the control alternatives?"}, {"title": "Pauli, Hadamard, and rotation gates", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-2-1", "text": "8.2.1 Pauli, Hadamard, and rotation gates § The basic matrices are \\[X=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\quad Z=\\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix},\\quad H=\\frac1{\\sqrt2}\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\1&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\\] Also \\(Y=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\\\i&0\\end{pmatrix}\\). For any Pauli matrix \\(P\\), define \\(R_P(\\theta)=\\cos(\\theta/2)I-i\\sin(\\theta/2)P\\). Since \\(P^2=I\\), this is unitary . The phase gates \\(S=\\operatorname{diag}(1,i)\\) and \\(T=\\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{i\\pi/4})\\) provide useful fixed relative phases. These formulas define the gates without requiring calculus or matrix exponentials. Predict first. Starting from \\(\\ket0\\), compare applying \\(H\\) twice with applying \\(H\\), then \\(Z\\), then \\(H\\). Predict both final measurement distributions before trying the gates. Experiment: a gate changes amplitudes Start in \\(\\ket{0}\\). Apply \\(H\\) twice, then compare \\(HZH\\) with a single \\(X\\). Orange marks a negative amplitude; the bar length shows its squared magnitude. Apply H Apply X Apply Z Reset to \\(\\ket0\\) State: \\(1\\ket0+0\\ket1\\) Choose a gate to change the state. This experiment uses real amplitudes. It illustrates X, Z, and H, not every possible qubit state. After trying it: compare your explanation \\(H\\) followed by \\(H\\) returns \\(\\ket0\\), so 0 is certain. \\(H\\), \\(Z\\), \\(H\\) produces \\(\\ket1\\), so 1 is certain. \\(Z\\) changes a relative sign that the final \\(H\\) converts into a different outcome. \\(X\\) exchanges basis labels; \\(Z\\) changes one sign; \\(H\\) changes basis. Their action on arbitrary superpositions follows by linearity. Problems What does the \\(X\\) gate do to \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\)? Compare \\(Z\\ket0\\) with \\(Z\\ket+\\). Which input makes the relative-sign change apparent? Applying \\(Z\\) to \\(\\ket0\\) changes nothing observable, but placing \\(Z\\) between two Hadamards changes the final outcome. Why is testing a gate on one input insufficient to establish that the gate has no effect?"}, {"title": "Controlled and entangling gates", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-2-2", "text": "8.2.2 Controlled and entangling gates § A controlled-U is \\(\\ket0\\bra0\\otimes I+\\ket1\\bra1\\otimes U\\). It retains the control and conditionally transforms the target. CNOT is controlled-X; controlled-Z changes only the sign of \\(\\ket{11}\\). Conjugating the target of CNOT by \\(H\\) gives controlled-Z because \\(HXH=Z\\). Not every controlled operation creates entanglement on every input, but CNOT can do so on \\(\\ket+\\ket0\\). Arbitrary local gates alone preserve product structure; an entangling operation is needed for general joint transformations. Hadamards on the target convert controlled-X to controlled-Z. Problems Which qubit changes in a CNOT operation? Compare CNOT on \\(\\ket0\\ket0\\) with CNOT on \\(\\ket+\\ket0\\). Write both outputs and identify which is entangled. A controlled gate is capable of entangling some inputs but leaves others as product states. What features of the control and target preparations decide which happens? Test CNOT on a basis control and on a superposed control."}, {"title": "Oracle and reflection circuits", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-2-3", "text": "8.2.3 Oracle and reflection circuits § A phase mark for a particular bit string can be built by flipping its zero positions, applying a phase flip controlled on all ones, and undoing those flips. A computed predicate can instead use compute–phase–uncompute. For uniform search, \\(D=H^{\\otimes n}(2\\ket{0^n}\\bra{0^n}-I)H^{\\otimes n}\\). The two-qubit drawing uses \\(X\\) gates and controlled-Z to realize the zero-state phase flip, producing \\(-D\\), which differs only by a harmless global phase in an uncontrolled Grover iteration . A controlled use would require tracking that phase explicitly. The oracle and reflection are decomposed here for one explicit predicate; a general predicate needs its own circuit. Problems Why are temporary workspace wires included in an oracle circuit? An implementation returns the correct answer but leaves a record \\(g(x)\\) in workspace. State the extra cleanup required before input branches can interfere without that record. When replacing an oracle box by a circuit, why must you check the final workspace as well as the output bits? Could a circuit have the correct truth table on its output and still spoil the intended quantum algorithm?"}, {"title": "Limits of local operations", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-3-1", "text": "8.3.1 Limits of local operations § A sequence made entirely of one-qubit gates has the form \\(U_1\\otimes\\cdots\\otimes U_n\\). On a product input it produces another product state . It therefore cannot prepare a Bell state from \\(\\ket{00}\\), regardless of how many local gates are used. This argument establishes a structural limitation without counting gates. Universal circuit construction needs a way for information in different subsystems to affect the joint state coherently. Applying \\(A\\) to one qubit and \\(B\\) to the other gives \\((A\\ket\\psi)\\otimes(B\\ket\\phi)\\). Starting with a product, local unitary gates alone cannot create entanglement. Problems Can local unitary gates create entanglement from a product state? Apply \\(H\\otimes I\\) to \\(\\ket0\\ket0\\) and write the result as a product of two one-qubit states. Why can local unitary operations change the appearance of an entangled state without removing its entanglement? Use the inverse operations to test what would follow if they could turn it into a product state."}, {"title": "Entangling gates and universality", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-3-2", "text": "8.3.2 Entangling gates and universality § Arbitrary single-qubit unitaries together with CNOT form a universal gate set for finite-qubit unitaries. One route to the theorem decomposes a matrix into two-level unitaries, each acting on a pair of basis vectors, and then implements those using controlled operations and local rotations. We state the synthesis theorem rather than prove every decomposition here. Its scope is expressibility, not efficiency: a generic operation on \\(n\\) qubits requires exponentially many parameters and generally many gates. Structured algorithmic operations are valuable because their descriptions permit economical constructions. Further detail: Barenco et al.: Elementary gates for quantum computation . Arbitrary one-qubit unitaries together with CNOT suffice to construct arbitrary finite-register unitaries. A chosen finite gate alphabet instead generally requires approximation. Problems What does a universal gate set allow us to construct or approximate? A collection contains only single-qubit gates. Starting from \\(\\ket{00}\\), can it prepare a Bell state? Use the product-state form to explain. Why does the availability of an entangling gate need to be considered together with the allowed one-qubit gates? What is missing from the claim that any collection containing one entangling operation is automatically universal?"}, {"title": "Exact synthesis and approximation", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-3-3", "text": "8.3.3 Exact synthesis and approximation § A finite set such as Clifford+T generates a dense collection of unitaries, enabling approximation to any desired accuracy; it cannot express every continuously parameterized unitary exactly with a finite word. A useful target is operator-norm error \\(\\|U-V\\|\\leq\\epsilon\\), which bounds the output-vector error for every unit input. If a circuit contains \\(L\\) approximated gates, errors can be budgeted so their sum is at most the desired tolerance. Precision belongs in the resource analysis: a gate count without its accuracy requirement is incomplete. The task is to find a finite circuit \\(V\\) close enough to a target \\(U\\). The tolerance controls acceptable error and affects the required circuit length. Problems What distinguishes exact synthesis from approximate synthesis? A circuit’s error is at most 0.01. Does this meet a requested tolerance of 0.1? Does it meet 0.001? Why does approximating every desired operation arbitrarily closely differ from expressing every operation exactly with finitely many gates? What additional question about approximation cost matters to computation?"}, {"title": "Gate count, depth, and workspace", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-4-1", "text": "8.4.1 Gate count, depth, and workspace § Gate count counts elementary operations, depth counts sequential layers that cannot be run in parallel, and width counts qubits, including ancillas. These resources can trade off: extra workspace may reduce depth, while repeated recomputation may save space. Clean ancillas must be initialized and usually restored for reuse. A uniform circuit family has a classical procedure generating the circuit for each input length; efficiency requires that construction to be efficient too. Otherwise a short circuit might conceal difficult work in its specification. Four independent gates on four wires can occupy one layer. Four dependent gates on one wire require four layers. Parallelism may use more workspace. Problems What is the difference between gate count and circuit depth? Four one-qubit gates act on four different qubits with no dependencies. Find their gate count and minimum depth, assuming parallel execution is available. Two circuits use the same number of gates but have different dependencies. Why might their running times differ? What other resource could increase when you try to reduce their depth?"}, {"title": "Connectivity and data preparation", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-4-2", "text": "8.4.2 Connectivity and data preparation § An abstract circuit may connect any pair of qubits, while a device permits only nearby interactions. Routing can add SWAP gates, count, and depth. Preparing an arbitrary list of amplitudes may itself require work proportional to the list size; it cannot be assumed free because the final register is small. Likewise, the arithmetic that implements a predicate may outweigh the number of oracle calls. A resource estimate should specify gate set, connectivity, input preparation, and output extraction before translating an asymptotic advantage into an implementation claim. On a three-site line only neighboring sites interact directly. To act on logical qubits A and C, swap A next to C, apply the gate, and restore the placement if needed. Problems What does hardware connectivity specify? Three qubits lie in a line with interactions only between neighbors. Which pair cannot interact directly, and what kind of extra operation could bring its information together? A circuit assumes that two distant qubits can interact. How does restricted hardware connectivity change the operations actually required? Why should loading the input also be counted before claiming an end-to-end advantage?"}, {"title": "Error accumulation and reliability", "chapter": "Quantum Circuits and Universality", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#topic-8-4-3", "text": "8.4.3 Error accumulation and reliability § Small coherent gate errors and stochastic noise can accumulate across a long circuit. Approximation error in ideal synthesis is different from physical noise, although both affect predictions. Reliable computation uses encoded logical information, syndrome extraction, and correction or fault-tolerant control. The no-cloning theorem does not forbid error correction: quantum codes encode one logical state into correlations, not independent readable copies. This book does not develop a noise model or threshold theorem. It identifies reliability as an additional resource requirement beyond proving an ideal algorithm correct. For ideal and implemented unitary gates with operator-norm error at most ε per gate, a telescoping bound gives total error at most mε. The bound is not a claim that all errors add with the same sign in a particular run. Problems What does a per-gate error bound describe? Using the bound that errors add, estimate the total error bound for 100 gates each with error at most 0.0001. Why can a small error in each gate become important in a long circuit? What assumptions would justify adding error bounds, and why should that worst-case bound not be mistaken for a prediction of the actual error in every run?"}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-1", "text": "Do \\(H\\) then \\(Z\\) and \\(Z\\) then \\(H\\) have the same action on \\(\\ket0\\)? No. \\(H\\) then \\(Z\\) gives \\(\\ket-\\); \\(Z\\) then \\(H\\) gives \\(\\ket+\\). Matrix multiplication order must match the circuit’s temporal order."}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-2", "text": "Show that \\(H\\) on the target, CNOT, then \\(H\\) on the target implements controlled-\\(Z\\). For control 0, the target undergoes \\(H\\cdot I\\cdot H=I\\). For control 1, it undergoes \\(H\\cdot X\\cdot H=Z\\). Only \\(\\ket{11}\\) gains a minus sign, so the joint operation is controlled-\\(Z\\). Linearity extends the result to superpositions."}, {"title": "Quantum Circuits and Universality — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "08-circuits-universality.html#exercise-3", "text": "A query algorithm uses \\(q\\) oracle calls. Each compiled oracle needs \\(g\\) elementary gates. What bound on total gates follows from \\(q\\) alone? No total gate bound follows from \\(q\\) alone. If each call costs \\(g\\) gates, the calls contribute \\(qg\\) gates, plus other operations. If \\(g\\) depends on input size or precision, it must remain in the analysis. Query savings alone do not establish a complete implementation advantage."}, {"title": "Input size and success criteria", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-1-1", "text": "9.1.1 Input size and success criteria § A comparison begins with the same task, encoding, input size, permitted access, and success criterion. For an integer \\(N\\), binary input length is of order \\(\\log N\\), so a procedure taking \\(N\\) steps is exponential in that length. Distinguish worst-case, average-case, and expected costs; an expectation needs a stated distribution or algorithmic randomness. Query models also specify what one request returns. Changing the interface can change the problem, not merely the method used to solve it. Binary encoding uses logarithmically many bits. A cost proportional to the integer’s value can therefore be exponential in the number of input bits. Problems What is meant by input length for a binary integer? Write 8 in binary and count its bits. Compare the value 8 with the input length. An integer’s value can be exponentially larger than the number of bits used to write it. Why must an efficiency claim identify the encoding? How might the same running-time formula be classified differently for binary and unary inputs?"}, {"title": "Asymptotic notation", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-1-2", "text": "9.1.2 Asymptotic notation § For eventually nonnegative functions, \\(T(n)=O(g(n))\\) means an eventual upper bound \\(T(n)\\leq Cg(n)\\) for some constant \\(C>0\\). The notation \\(\\Omega(g(n))\\) gives a corresponding lower bound ; \\(\\Theta(g(n))\\) gives both. For example, \\(3n^2+7n+2=\\Theta(n^2)\\). These notations suppress constants, not the input encoding or the model. Polynomial bounds describe powers of input length; exponential bounds such as \\(2^n\\) grow much faster. A polynomial exponent can still make a method impractical at relevant sizes. The linear algorithm with a large constant eventually wins, but not for every input size. Big-O notation describes growth, not a complete timing prediction. Problems What does an \\(O(n^2)\\) upper bound say about growth? For \\(n\\ge1\\), bound \\(3n^2+2n\\) by a constant times \\(n^2\\). Give a suitable constant. Two algorithms have costs \\(n^2\\) and a million times \\(n\\). What does asymptotic comparison tell you, and what does it leave open for inputs you can actually run? Why are both questions worth asking?"}, {"title": "Query, gate, and space complexity", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-1-3", "text": "9.1.3 Query, gate, and space complexity § Query complexity counts oracle uses, gate complexity counts elementary operations, and space complexity counts memory. A quantum query advantage may survive or disappear when translated into total gate and preparation cost. Physical runtime also depends on scheduling, error correction, and device constraints. State which level a bound concerns. For unstructured search, the quadratic query reduction is a precise theorem. It is not a theorem that searching any stored dataset on any quantum device is quadratically faster end to end. These totals count only the gates inside query calls. Preparation and other gates must still be added for a full comparison. Problems What does query complexity count? Algorithm A uses 5 queries of 100 gates each; B uses 10 queries of 10 gates each. Compare their query counts and the gates used by their queries. How could one algorithm use fewer queries but more elementary gates than another? Why is there no meaningful claim of “fewer resources” until the resource being compared is named?"}, {"title": "P, NP, and reductions", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-2-1", "text": "9.2.1 P, NP, and reductions § P consists of decision problems solvable deterministically in polynomial time. NP consists of those whose yes instances have polynomial-size witnesses verifiable in polynomial time. Equivalently, a nondeterministic machine can guess a witness and verify it; this is not a claim that a physical random guess will probably find one. A polynomial-time reduction from \\(A\\) to \\(B\\) transforms instances so that a solver for \\(B\\) yields a solver for \\(A\\) with polynomial overhead. It compares difficulty without claiming equal runtimes. Whether P equals NP remains unresolved. If task \\(A\\) reduces efficiently to task \\(B\\), an efficient solver for \\(B\\) would yield one for \\(A\\). The arrow describes converting instances, not proving that such a solver exists. Problems In the definition of NP, what is checked efficiently? A proposed factor \\(d\\) of \\(N\\) is supplied. Describe how division checks this proposal without finding \\(d\\) yourself. Checking a proposed solution efficiently is different from finding one efficiently. What would an efficient reduction allow you to transfer between problems, and why would that still not by itself settle whether P equals NP?"}, {"title": "BPP and BQP", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-2-2", "text": "9.2.2 BPP and BQP § BPP uses classical randomness and polynomial time, with error bounded by a constant below one half, conventionally one third. BQP uses uniformly generated polynomial-size quantum circuits with a comparable error bound. Repetition and suitable decision rules can reduce error when independent trials have a fixed gap above chance. Quantum circuits can implement classical randomized computation, giving \\(\\mathrm{BPP}\\subseteq\\mathrm{BQP}\\). It is not known that BQP contains all of NP, or that every BQP problem lies outside efficient classical computation. These are classes of decision tasks; factoring is usually posed as a function task. Both classes concern polynomial-time decision procedures with a bounded error on every allowed input. The computational model differs. Problems Which model of computation distinguishes BPP from BQP? An algorithm must succeed with probability at least \\(2/3\\) on every input. Does success on 99 out of 100 tested inputs establish that guarantee for all inputs? Explain. Why must the error guarantee hold for every allowed input rather than only on average over a convenient test set? What would experiments on a small collection of instances fail to establish about membership in BPP or BQP?"}, {"title": "Factoring and search", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-2-3", "text": "9.2.3 Factoring and search § Quantum period-finding methods give polynomial-time factoring algorithms in the binary input length, although their derivation lies beyond this book. No polynomial-time classical factoring algorithm is known; this is not a classical lower bound proving exponential advantage. Grover search, in contrast, has a proved quadratic separation in a specified unstructured query setting. These examples illustrate two kinds of evidence: an algorithm compared with currently known competitors, and an optimality statement within a restricted model. Neither implies that quantum computers accelerate every task. Factoring uses arithmetic structure; unstructured search assumes only oracle access to a predicate. A lower bound for the latter is not a lower bound for every structured problem. Problems Is unstructured search assumed to provide useful relations between different candidates? Compare factoring an integer with searching an arbitrary list. Identify the arithmetic structure available in the first task. Why can an efficient quantum factoring algorithm and a quadratic quantum search improvement coexist without contradiction? Which structural difference prevents an unstructured-search lower bound from automatically applying to factoring?"}, {"title": "Upper and lower bounds", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-3-1", "text": "9.3.1 Upper and lower bounds § An upper bound is established by an algorithm and a cost analysis. A lower bound must constrain every algorithm allowed by the model, including ones nobody has invented. Thus a slow known method does not establish a lower bound. Restricting to query access makes some general arguments possible: algorithms may perform arbitrary input-independent processing, but the hidden input influences them only through counted queries. A bound in this model can be strong and useful while saying nothing by itself about the cost of implementing a query. A construction proves that a task can be solved within a cost. A lower bound must cover every permitted algorithm in the stated model. Problems Does an upper bound describe one available method or rule out every faster method? Someone gives an \\(O(n^2)\\) algorithm and later someone gives an \\(O(n)\\) algorithm. Which upper bounds are still valid? Why does inventing a faster algorithm refute a proposed lower bound but never refute a valid upper bound? What different burdens of proof do these two kinds of claim carry?"}, {"title": "Unstructured search optimality", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-3-2", "text": "9.3.2 Unstructured search optimality § For finding a unique marked item among \\(N\\) candidates with constant success probability, quantum algorithms require \\(\\Omega(\\sqrt N)\\) queries. Grover’s \\(O(\\sqrt N)\\) algorithm matches this, so the query complexity is \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\). Intuitively, a query can change the state substantially only through amplitude placed on the marked location; averaged over possible locations, that amplitude is limited. A hybrid argument makes this accumulation precise. We state the lower-bound theorem here; the geometric derivation of Grover alone proves its upper bound, not its optimality. For one marked item and constant success in the unstructured quantum query model, Grover’s \\(O(\\sqrt N)\\) upper bound and the \\(\\Omega(\\sqrt N)\\) lower bound give \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\) query complexity. Problems What resource does the standard unstructured-search lower bound count? If extra structure lets a classical algorithm avoid inspecting unrelated items, is it still the same unstructured access model? Explain. An optimality theorem rules out faster unstructured search in a particular query model. Which part of the problem would have to change for a faster method to be possible without contradicting that theorem?"}, {"title": "Acceptance polynomials and approximate degree (optional)", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-3-3", "text": "9.3.3 Acceptance polynomials and approximate degree (optional) § Optional derivation · polynomial lower bounds An alternative method represents acceptance probability as a polynomial in Boolean oracle bits. After \\(T\\) standard queries, amplitudes have degree at most \\(T\\), and probabilities degree at most \\(2T\\). A bounded-error algorithm must therefore produce a low-degree polynomial approximating the desired Boolean function on every Boolean input. If the function’s approximate degree is \\(d\\), then \\(T\\geq d/2\\). The approximate degree of OR on \\(N\\) bits is \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\), yielding a matching lower bound for detecting a marked item. Appendix E explains the representation; the approximation theorem is an additional mathematical result. Further detail: Beals et al.: Quantum Lower Bounds by Polynomials . The query model limits acceptance degree to \\(2T\\). If approximating the requested function requires degree \\(d\\), then \\(2T\\ge d\\). The approximate-degree theorem is a separate ingredient. Problems After \\(T\\) queries, what is the maximum degree bound on an acceptance probability? A function has approximate degree \\(d=10\\). Use \\(T\\ge d/2\\) to obtain a query lower bound. Why is a low-degree polynomial representation useful for proving a lower bound only when paired with a theorem about the function being approximated? Which half of that argument is supplied by the query model, and which is a separate mathematical result?"}, {"title": "Algorithmic mechanisms and assumptions", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-4-1", "text": "9.4.1 Algorithmic mechanisms and assumptions § The algorithms in this book use coherent transformations to make a requested property affect outcome probabilities. Deutsch’s algorithm turns equality of function values into relative phase and then a definite bit. Amplitude amplification rotates a prepared state toward a successful subspace. Their advantages require specified oracles, controllable inverse operations where used, and a suitable final measurement. Large state spaces provide room for these constructions, but size alone is not their explanation. Correctness is established by the evolution and readout calculation. An advantage claim must include the access assumptions and success criterion. Expensive preparation or readout can dominate a reduction in oracle calls. Problems Name two assumptions that should accompany a claimed quantum speedup. An algorithm makes fewer queries but requires costly input preparation. Which additional cost must be included before comparing full implementations? If an algorithm’s speedup relies on inexpensive state preparation and oracle access, what happens when those assumptions fail? How would you separate a correct conditional advantage from an unsupported claim about a practical implementation?"}, {"title": "Computability and readout limitations", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-4-2", "text": "9.4.2 Computability and readout limitations § Quantum computation does not remove the halting obstruction, permit arbitrary cloning, or expose all amplitudes through one measurement. It also does not eliminate the cost of encoding inputs, implementing operations, or protecting states from noise. These constraints are part of the model, not qualifications appended after the algorithm. A useful claim names the task, mechanism, success guarantee, and resources. The same discipline that distinguished a pattern from a proof at the start now distinguishes a plausible speedup from an established one. A faster implementation does not turn an undecidable task into a decidable one, nor make all amplitudes available from a single measurement. Problems What is the difference between computability and efficiency? A computer runs every step a million times faster. Does a program that never halts now halt? Explain. Why would a dramatic speedup on every problem a quantum computer can solve still not provide a general halting decider? What distinction between efficiency and computability survives any such improvement?"}, {"title": "Period finding and error correction: further topics", "chapter": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#topic-9-4-3", "text": "9.4.3 Period finding and error correction: further topics § Period finding develops a different use of interference : a Fourier transform converts periodic structure into measurable frequency information. Quantum error correction addresses the reliability of long computations through encoded subspaces and measured syndromes. Both build on the state, operator, measurement, and circuit language developed here, but require additional constructions and proofs. They are natural continuations rather than prerequisites to the present algorithms. The current foundation is complete enough to identify what those later chapters must explain: a mechanism, a correct readout, and an honest resource bound. Period finding concerns making structure observable; error correction concerns protecting a computation from faults. A useful large computation needs both suitable algorithms and sufficiently reliable operations. Problems Which further topic protects computations against noise? Match “extract periodic structure” and “preserve encoded information despite errors” to period finding and error correction. Period finding concerns extracting structure; error correction concerns preserving a computation despite noise. Why does progress on either problem not automatically solve the other? What must a useful large computation achieve on both fronts?"}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Quick check", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-1", "text": "An algorithm uses \\(O(N)\\) steps on an integer \\(N\\) written in binary. Is that polynomial in input length? Not in general. The input length is of order \\(\\log_2 N\\). A number of steps proportional to \\(N\\) can grow exponentially with that length."}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Worked problem", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-2", "text": "One algorithm uses at most \\(3n^2+7n+2\\) operations. What asymptotic bound does this give, and does it prove that every algorithm needs quadratic time? It gives an \\(O(n^2)\\) upper bound for this algorithm. If the displayed expression is its actual cost, that cost is \\(\\Theta(n^2)\\). Neither statement proves a lower bound for all algorithms solving the task."}, {"title": "Computational Complexity and Quantum Advantage — Extension", "chapter": "Exercises", "url": "09-complexity-advantage.html#exercise-3", "text": "Suppose a quantum search uses \\(\\Theta(\\sqrt N)\\) queries, but each query requires \\(\\Theta(N)\\) gates in a chosen implementation. What do the query and gate counts say? Those calls contribute \\(\\Theta(N^{3/2})\\) gates to that implementation, before other costs. The quadratic query improvement remains a statement about oracle access. It does not show that this implementation beats a classical end-to-end method, nor rule out a better oracle implementation."}, {"title": "Automata and Memory", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-a", "text": "A. Automata and Memory A finite automaton stores one of finitely many control states while reading an input. Two states suffice for parity: reading one swaps “even” and “odd,” and reading zero leaves the state unchanged. Exact matching of arbitrarily many opening and closing symbols needs more memory. For the language \\(\\{a^nb^n:n\\geq0\\}\\), two different long prefixes of a’s must reach the same state in any fixed finite automaton. Appending the number of b’s matching one prefix then forces the same decision on one valid and one invalid string. This state-collision argument proves the limitation. A stack distinguishes arbitrarily many pending symbols. Push once for each \\(a\\), then pop once for each \\(b\\); accept when input and stack finish together in the correct order. A pushdown automaton formalizes this memory. A linear-bounded automaton has writable tape bounded by a constant multiple of input length. A Turing machine removes that fixed bound. More memory changes the configurations available, not the meaning of an individual step. These models explain different language classes; they are not successive hardware generations. Reading \\(aabb\\) pushes for each \\(a\\) and pops for each \\(b\\). The empty stack at the end certifies matching counts in this example; checking order is also required. Problems What kind of memory does a pushdown automaton add to a finite automaton? Process \\(aabb\\) by pushing once for each \\(a\\) and popping once for each \\(b\\). State the stack size after each symbol. Why does a collision between the states reached by two different prefixes prove a memory limitation? What continuation exposes that collision, and why does the argument depend on allowing arbitrarily long inputs?"}, {"title": "Grammars and Lambda Calculus", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-b", "text": "B. Grammars and Lambda Calculus A grammar specifies a start symbol, terminal symbols, nonterminal symbols, and replacement rules. The rules \\(S\\to aSb\\) and \\(S\\to\\epsilon\\) generate exactly matching a’s and b’s. Applying the first rule twice and then the second gives \\(aabb\\). Generation asks what can be produced; recognition asks whether a supplied string belongs to the generated set. Regular grammars correspond to finite automata, context-free grammars to pushdown automata, context-sensitive grammars to linearly bounded machines, and unrestricted grammars to Turing-recognizable languages, with the usual conventions about the empty string. Lambda calculus describes computation by function application and substitution. The reduction \\((\\lambda x.t)u\\to t[x:=u]\\) replaces free occurrences of \\(x\\) in \\(t\\) by \\(u\\), renaming bound variables when necessary to avoid capture. For example, \\((\\lambda x.x)u\\to u\\). A Church numeral \\(n\\) is a function that applies a supplied function \\(n\\) times to a supplied starting value. Thus three is \\(\\lambda f.\\lambda x.f(f(f(x)))\\). Functions alone can encode data and iteration. Agreement with Turing computability is a mathematical equivalence between formal models; it supports, rather than defines away, the broader Church–Turing thesis. Apply \\(S\\to aSb\\) twice, then \\(S\\to\\) empty string. This demonstrates generation of \\(aabb\\); it does not yet give a procedure for rejecting arbitrary strings. Problems What string results from applying \\(S\\to aSb\\) once and then \\(S\\to\\) empty string? Generate \\(aabb\\) from \\(S\\) using the two rules. Write each intermediate string. Why does being able to generate every valid string not automatically give a procedure that rejects every invalid string? How does this distinction connect grammars back to proof search and decision?"}, {"title": "Linear Algebra Reference", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-c", "text": "C. Linear Algebra Reference For complex coordinates, use \\(\\braket uv=u^\\dagger v\\), where \\(u^\\dagger=(u^*)^T\\). The norm is \\(\\sqrt{u^\\dagger u}\\). An orthonormal basis matrix \\(B\\) satisfies \\(B^\\dagger B=I\\), and converts old coordinates to new by \\(B^\\dagger\\). Matrix products act right to left on column vectors. The adjoint reverses their order: \\((AB)^\\dagger=B^\\dagger A^\\dagger\\). A unitary preserves overlaps; a Hermitian operator equals its adjoint; an orthogonal projector satisfies \\(P^2=P=P^\\dagger\\). These are different properties. Tensor products obey \\((A\\otimes B)(u\\otimes v)=Au\\otimes Bv\\) and \\(\\braket{u\\otimes v}{x\\otimes y}=\\braket ux\\braket vy\\). In the binary ordering used in this book, the leftmost qubit is the most significant label. Operator norm is \\(\\|A\\|=\\max_{\\|v\\|=1}\\|Av\\|\\). It is useful for uniform error bounds because \\(\\|(U-V)v\\|\\leq\\|U-V\\|\\) for every unit vector. These identities provide reference material; their operational meanings are introduced where used in the chapters. For the complex vector \\((3,4i)\\), the squared norm is \\(9+16=25\\). Dividing by five gives a unit vector; the imaginary coordinate contributes positively to its norm. Problems How do you find the norm of a complex vector? Calculate the norm of \\((3,4i)^T\\), then normalize the vector. A matrix is a representation, while a transformation acts on vectors independently of coordinates. Which identities in this reference ensure that changing coordinates consistently preserves lengths, overlaps, and predictions?"}, {"title": "Mixed States and General Measurements", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-d", "text": "D. Mixed States and General Measurements A density matrix records a preparation that need not be pure. An ensemble preparing \\(\\ket{\\psi_r}\\) with probability \\(q_r\\) has \\(\\rho=\\sum_r q_r\\ket{\\psi_r}\\bra{\\psi_r}\\). It is positive semidefinite and has trace one. A pure state has \\(\\rho=\\ket\\psi\\bra\\psi\\); different ensembles can yield the same density matrix and therefore the same measurement predictions. The Born rule is \\(p(j)=\\operatorname{tr}(P_j\\rho)\\), and unitary evolution gives \\(U\\rho U^\\dagger\\). For a joint system, the reduced state is \\(\\rho_A=\\operatorname{tr}_B\\rho_{AB}\\): sum the \\(B\\) diagonal blocks in a product basis. Tracing one half of the Bell state gives \\(I/2\\). A trace-preserving local operation on \\(B\\), with operators \\(M_r\\) satisfying \\(\\sum_rM_r^\\dagger M_r=I\\), leaves \\(A\\)’s reduced state unchanged when outcomes are ignored. This follows by inserting the sum into the partial trace. Conditioning on \\(r\\) changes the description only for someone who knows \\(r\\). A general measurement has positive effects \\(E_j\\) summing to identity and probabilities \\(\\operatorname{tr}(E_j\\rho)\\). Effects specify probabilities but not the complete state update; measurement operators provide that additional information. For example, to distinguish either \\(\\ket0\\) or \\(\\ket+\\), choose \\(E_0=c\\ket-\\bra-\\), \\(E_+=c\\ket1\\bra1\\), and \\(E_?=I-E_0-E_+\\), with \\(c=1/(1+1/\\sqrt2)\\). The first outcome is impossible for \\(\\ket+\\), the second for \\(\\ket0\\), and the remaining outcome is inconclusive. Positivity of \\(E_?\\) follows because the largest eigenvalue of \\(\\ket-\\bra-+\\ket1\\bra1\\) is \\(1+1/\\sqrt2\\). Certainty when conclusive is purchased by sometimes declining an answer. An equal mixture of \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\) and an equal mixture of \\(\\ket+\\) and \\(\\ket-\\) both yield \\(I/2\\). No measurement on the qubit alone identifies which ensemble was used. Problems What two properties must a density matrix satisfy? Form the density matrix for an equal mixture of \\(\\ket0\\) and \\(\\ket1\\). Check its trace and computational-basis probabilities. Two different ensembles can produce the same density matrix. What could any measurement on the prepared system reveal about which ensemble was used? How would access to a separate preparation record change the question?"}, {"title": "Polynomial Lower Bounds", "chapter": "Appendices", "url": "appendices.html#appendix-e", "text": "E. Polynomial Lower Bounds In the Boolean query model, the hidden input is a string \\(z\\in\\{0,1\\}^N\\). A query transforms \\(\\ket{i,b}\\) to \\(\\ket{i,b\\oplus z_i}\\). Each output amplitude is a combination of previous amplitudes multiplied by \\(z_i\\) or \\(1-z_i\\). Starting from input-independent amplitudes, induction shows that \\(T\\) queries produce amplitudes of polynomial degree at most \\(T\\) in the input bits. Input-independent unitaries form linear combinations and do not increase this degree. An acceptance probability sums squared magnitudes of accepting amplitudes, so its degree is at most 2T. Restricting to Boolean inputs allows powers \\(z_i^k\\) with positive \\(k\\) to be replaced by \\(z_i\\). The approximate degree of a Boolean function is the least degree of a real polynomial approximating it to a chosen constant error below one half on every Boolean input. Hence query complexity is at least half that degree. Proving OR has approximate degree of order \\(\\sqrt N\\) requires an approximation-theoretic argument beyond this short derivation. The polynomial representation and that theorem together yield the search-decision lower bound ; neither follows from observing that known algorithms are slow. Starting from amplitudes independent of hidden input bits, each Boolean query raises their degree by at most one. Squaring magnitudes gives the factor of two for acceptance probabilities. Problems How much can one query increase the degree bound on an amplitude? For \\(T=3\\) queries, give the degree bounds on amplitudes and acceptance probabilities. Explain why the latter can be twice as large. Why do input-independent gates fail to increase polynomial degree while oracle queries can increase it? Which assumption about access to the hidden input would fail if the starting amplitudes already depended on its bits?"}, {"title": "Adjoint", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#adjoint", "text": "The conjugate transpose of a matrix, written with a dagger. It is not generally the inverse."}, {"title": "Algorithm", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#algorithm", "text": "A finite set of precise instructions for carrying out a task. Whether it terminates on every allowed input must be established."}, {"title": "Amplitude", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#amplitude", "text": "A complex coefficient used to describe a quantum state or transition. The squared magnitude gives the corresponding probability in the specified measurement basis."}, {"title": "Amplitude amplification", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#amplitude-amplification", "text": "A sequence of reflections that increases the amplitude in a designated successful subspace, given suitable preparation and inverse operations."}, {"title": "Bell state", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#bell-state", "text": "One of four maximally entangled two-qubit basis states. The book begins with the equal superposition of 00 and 11."}, {"title": "Boolean function", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#boolean-function", "text": "A function whose inputs and outputs are bits. A truth table specifies its value for every input."}, {"title": "BQP", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#bqp", "text": "The class of decision problems solvable by uniform polynomial-size quantum circuits with bounded error."}, {"title": "Circuit depth", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#circuit-depth", "text": "The number of sequential layers of operations when compatible operations can run in parallel."}, {"title": "Computability", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#computability", "text": "Whether a task can be performed by an effective procedure, without imposing an efficiency requirement."}, {"title": "Consistency", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#consistency", "text": "A formal theory is consistent if its rules cannot prove both a statement and its negation."}, {"title": "Decision problem", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#decision-problem", "text": "A problem whose required answer is yes or no for each allowed input."}, {"title": "Entanglement", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#entanglement", "text": "For a pure joint state, the inability to write it as a product of subsystem states. Mixed-state entanglement requires a broader definition."}, {"title": "Global phase", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#global-phase", "text": "A common phase factor multiplying an entire state vector. It leaves all measurement probabilities unchanged."}, {"title": "Grover iteration", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#grover-iteration", "text": "A phase reversal on marked items followed by a reflection about the prepared uniform state in the standard search algorithm."}, {"title": "Halting problem", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#halting-problem", "text": "The task of deciding, for every program and input, whether the program eventually stops. No algorithm solves it in full generality."}, {"title": "Hermitian", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#hermitian", "text": "An operator equal to its adjoint. Its eigenvalues are real and its eigenspaces can describe a projective measurement."}, {"title": "Inner product", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#inner-product", "text": "A scalar obtained from two vectors by conjugating the coordinates of the first and summing their products with the second. It measures their overlap."}, {"title": "Interference", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#interference", "text": "The addition of amplitudes for indistinguishable alternatives, which can increase or decrease an outcome probability."}, {"title": "Lower bound", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#lower-bound", "text": "A limit on the resources required by every algorithm within a specified model, rather than the cost of one construction."}, {"title": "NAND", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#nand", "text": "The Boolean operation that returns zero exactly when both input bits are one. NAND gates suffice to build every Boolean function."}, {"title": "Normalization", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#normalization", "text": "The requirement that a state vector has squared norm one, so the probabilities of a complete basis measurement sum to one."}, {"title": "Oracle", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#oracle", "text": "A specified operation that provides access to a function. Counting its uses does not by itself account for the cost of building it."}, {"title": "Phase kickback", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#phase-kickback", "text": "When a controlled operation acts on a target eigenstate, its eigenvalue appears as a phase on the corresponding control component."}, {"title": "Product state", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#product-state", "text": "A pure joint state that can be written as a tensor product of one state for each subsystem."}, {"title": "Projective measurement", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#projective-measurement", "text": "A measurement described by mutually orthogonal projectors summing to the identity. Each outcome probability is the squared norm of the projected state."}, {"title": "Quantum advantage", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#quantum-advantage", "text": "An improvement over classical computation for a specified task, resource measure, and success guarantee. Its assumptions must be stated."}, {"title": "Quantum circuit", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#quantum-circuit", "text": "An ordered description of operations on quantum registers, with any measurements and classical control specified."}, {"title": "Query complexity", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#query-complexity", "text": "The number of oracle calls required for a task under a stated success criterion and oracle model."}, {"title": "Relative phase", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#relative-phase", "text": "The phase difference between two components of a state. It can affect probabilities after further operations."}, {"title": "Reversible", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#reversible", "text": "A transformation is reversible when its output determines its input uniquely on the allowed state space."}, {"title": "Superposition", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#superposition", "text": "A linear combination of basis states. Its components depend on the chosen basis; measurement does not reveal all coefficients at once."}, {"title": "Tensor product", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#tensor-product", "text": "The construction of a joint vector space from component spaces. Basis labels combine in pairs and dimensions multiply."}, {"title": "Turing machine", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#turing-machine", "text": "A mathematical model with a tape, a read/write head, and finitely many control states. A transition rule specifies each step."}, {"title": "Uncomputation", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#uncomputation", "text": "Applying the inverse of a computation to clear temporary workspace while retaining the needed result or phase."}, {"title": "Unitary", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#unitary", "text": "An operator whose adjoint is its inverse. Unitary transformations preserve inner products and describe closed-system quantum evolution."}, {"title": "Universal gate set", "chapter": "Glossary", "url": "glossary.html#universal-gate-set", "text": "A set of gates sufficient to construct arbitrary quantum operations in the stated model, exactly or to a specified approximation."}];
